List<Order> lowValue  = partition.get(false);

// teeing collector (Java 12+) — two collectors in one pass:
record Stats(BigDecimal total, long count) {}
Stats orderStats = orders.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.teeing(
        Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Order::getTotal, BigDecimal::add),
        Collectors.counting(),
        Stats::new
    ));
```

## When NOT to Use Streams

```java
// TOO COMPLEX — use loop instead:
// If you need to break out of the middle with complex state:
boolean found = false;
for (Order order : orders) {
    if (order.isValid() && processOrder(order)) {  // processOrder has side effects
        found = true;
        break;  // Clear intent
    }
}

// Avoid: stream with external state mutation:
int[] count = {0};  // Hack to mutate in lambda
orders.stream().filter(o -> {
    count[0]++;   // Side effect — not thread-safe, unclear intent
    return o.isActive();
}).collect(toList());
// Use: Collectors.counting() instead

// Simple loops are often MORE readable:
// Stream: orders.stream().filter(Order::isActive).findFirst().orElseThrow()
// Loop:   for (Order o : orders) { if (o.isActive()) return o; } throw ...
// Same performance, clearer for simple cases
```

## Production Reality

Streams shine for data transformation pipelines: convert a list of entities to DTOs, group by some criteria, aggregate totals. They're less appropriate for complex iteration with multiple exit conditions or significant side effects. A useful heuristic: if you need a `for` label or `break`, use a loop. If you're transforming data from one form to another, streams are likely cleaner.

## Type Safety with Generics

```java
// Without generics: ClassCastException at runtime
List list = new ArrayList();
list.add("hello");
list.add(42);  // No error at compile time!
String s = (String) list.get(1);  // ClassCastException at runtime!

// With generics: caught at compile time
List<String> strings = new ArrayList<>();
strings.add("hello");
// strings.add(42);  // COMPILE ERROR: int cannot be added to List<String>
String s2 = strings.get(0);  // No cast needed — type is known
```

## Type Erasure

```java
// These are THE SAME at runtime:
List<String> stringList = new ArrayList<>();
List<Integer> intList = new ArrayList<>();
System.out.println(stringList.getClass() == intList.getClass());  // true!
System.out.println(stringList.getClass().getName());  // java.util.ArrayList

// Cannot do at runtime:
List<String> ls = new ArrayList<>();
if (ls instanceof List<String>) { }  // COMPILE ERROR — erased
String[] arr = new String[10];
// T[] arr = new T[10];  // COMPILE ERROR — T erased

// Workaround with Class<T>:
public class TypedList<T> {
    private final Class<T> type;
    private final List<T> items = new ArrayList<>();

    public TypedList(Class<T> type) { this.type = type; }

    public boolean isType(Object obj) {
        return type.isInstance(obj);  // Works at runtime — Class retains type
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public T cast(Object obj) {
        return type.cast(obj);  // Checked cast — throws ClassCastException if wrong
    }
}
```

