@Configuration
@EnableCaching
public class CacheConfig {

    @Bean
    public RedisCacheManager cacheManager(RedisConnectionFactory connectionFactory) {
        // Per-cache TTL configuration:
        Map<String, RedisCacheConfiguration> cacheConfigs = new HashMap<>();

        // Product catalog: changes rarely → long TTL
        cacheConfigs.put("products", RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig()
            .entryTtl(Duration.ofHours(24))
            .serializeValuesWith(serializationPair()));  // Use JSON

        // User sessions: medium TTL
        cacheConfigs.put("userSessions", RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig()
            .entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(30))
            .serializeValuesWith(serializationPair()));

        // Exchange rates: change frequently → short TTL
        cacheConfigs.put("exchangeRates", RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig()
            .entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(5))
            .serializeValuesWith(serializationPair()));

        return RedisCacheManager.builder(connectionFactory)
            .cacheDefaults(RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig()
                .entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(10))  // Default
                .serializeValuesWith(serializationPair()))
            .withInitialCacheConfigurations(cacheConfigs)
            .build();
    }

    private RedisSerializationContext.SerializationPair<Object> serializationPair() {
        return RedisSerializationContext.SerializationPair
            .fromSerializer(new GenericJackson2JsonRedisSerializer());
    }
}
```

## @Cacheable, @CacheEvict, @CachePut

```java
@Service
public class ProductService {

    // @Cacheable: cache result; return cache on subsequent calls
    @Cacheable(value = "products", key = "#productId")
    public Product findById(Long productId) {
        log.info("Cache MISS — loading from DB for product {}", productId);
        return productRepository.findById(productId)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException("Product " + productId));
        // First call: hits DB, stores in Redis as: products::42 → {Product JSON}
        // Subsequent calls: returns from Redis, DB never called
    }

    // Conditional caching — don't cache non-existent:
    @Cacheable(value = "products", key = "#productId", unless = "#result == null")
    public Product findByIdOrNull(Long productId) {
        return productRepository.findById(productId).orElse(null);
    }

    // @CacheEvict: remove from cache when data changes
    @CacheEvict(value = "products", key = "#product.id")
    @Transactional
    public Product update(Product product) {
        return productRepository.save(product);
        // After save, Redis entry products::42 is deleted
        // Next findById(42) will reload from DB
    }

    // @CachePut: always calls method AND updates cache
    @CachePut(value = "products", key = "#product.id")
    @Transactional
    public Product updateAndCache(Product product) {
        return productRepository.save(product);
        // Different from @CacheEvict: updates cache with new value immediately
        // No stale window — cache has the latest value right away
    }

    // Evict entire cache region:
    @CacheEvict(value = "products", allEntries = true)
    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 * * * *")  // Hourly
    public void clearProductCache() {
        log.info("Clearing entire product cache");
    }
}
```

## Cache-Aside Pattern (Manual Control)

```java
@Service
public class InventoryService {

    private final RedisTemplate<String, InventoryStatus> redisTemplate;
    private final InventoryRepository inventoryRepository;

    public InventoryStatus getStatus(String productId) {
        String key = "inventory:" + productId;

        // 1. Check cache:
        InventoryStatus cached = redisTemplate.opsForValue().get(key);
        if (cached != null) {
            return cached;  // Cache HIT
        }

        // 2. Cache MISS — load from DB:
        InventoryStatus status = inventoryRepository.findByProductId(productId);

        // 3. Store in cache with TTL:
        redisTemplate.opsForValue().set(key, status, Duration.ofMinutes(5));

        return status;
    }

    public void updateInventory(String productId, int quantity) {
        inventoryRepository.updateQuantity(productId, quantity);

        // Invalidate cache:
        redisTemplate.delete("inventory:" + productId);
        // Next getStatus() will reload from DB
    }
}
```

## Distributed Lock with Redisson

```java
// Preventing duplicate processing:
@Service
public class PaymentService {

    private final RedissonClient redisson;

    public PaymentResult processPayment(String orderId, PaymentRequest request) {
        // Distributed lock: only one instance processes this order at a time
        RLock lock = redisson.getLock("payment-lock:" + orderId);

        try {
            // Try to acquire lock, wait max 5s, hold max 30s:
            boolean acquired = lock.tryLock(5, 30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
            if (!acquired) {
                throw new PaymentInProgressException("Payment already being processed");
            }

            // Check idempotency first:
            if (paymentRepository.existsByOrderId(orderId)) {
                return paymentRepository.findByOrderId(orderId);
            }

            return chargeAndSave(request);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            throw new PaymentException("Interrupted while acquiring lock");
        } finally {
            if (lock.isHeldByCurrentThread()) {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

Always serialize to JSON (not Java serialization) — JSON survives code refactoring and can be read externally for debugging. Monitor cache hit rate via Redis INFO stats or Spring Boot Actuator (`cache.gets` metric tagged with `result=hit/miss`). A hit rate below 80% suggests the TTL is too short or you're caching the wrong things. Watch Redis memory usage — set `maxmemory` and an eviction policy (`allkeys-lru` is safe for caches) so Redis doesn't run out of memory.

## Table Partitioning in PostgreSQL

```sql
-- RANGE PARTITIONING: partition by date
CREATE TABLE orders (
    id          BIGSERIAL,
    user_id     BIGINT NOT NULL,
    status      VARCHAR(20),
    total       DECIMAL(12,2),
    created_at  TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (id, created_at)  -- Partition key must be in PK
) PARTITION BY RANGE (created_at);

-- Create partitions per quarter:
CREATE TABLE orders_2024_q1 PARTITION OF orders
    FOR VALUES FROM ('2024-01-01') TO ('2024-04-01');

CREATE TABLE orders_2024_q2 PARTITION OF orders
    FOR VALUES FROM ('2024-04-01') TO ('2024-07-01');

CREATE TABLE orders_2024_q3 PARTITION OF orders
    FOR VALUES FROM ('2024-07-01') TO ('2024-10-01');

CREATE TABLE orders_2024_q4 PARTITION OF orders
    FOR VALUES FROM ('2024-10-01') TO ('2025-01-01');

-- Query automatically uses partition pruning:
SELECT * FROM orders
WHERE created_at BETWEEN '2024-04-01' AND '2024-06-30';
-- Only reads orders_2024_q2 — other partitions skipped

-- Archive old data: just drop the partition (instant, no DELETE cost)
DROP TABLE orders_2023_q1;
-- No full table scan, no undo log, just detach and drop

-- HASH PARTITIONING: distribute evenly
CREATE TABLE users (
    id      BIGSERIAL,
    email   VARCHAR(255),
    PRIMARY KEY (id)
) PARTITION BY HASH (id);

CREATE TABLE users_0 PARTITION OF users FOR VALUES WITH (MODULUS 4, REMAINDER 0);
CREATE TABLE users_1 PARTITION OF users FOR VALUES WITH (MODULUS 4, REMAINDER 1);
CREATE TABLE users_2 PARTITION OF users FOR VALUES WITH (MODULUS 4, REMAINDER 2);
CREATE TABLE users_3 PARTITION OF users FOR VALUES WITH (MODULUS 4, REMAINDER 3);
-- Each partition gets ~25% of users based on id hash
```

## Sharding — Multi-Server Split

```
SHARDING ARCHITECTURE:

 Application
     ↓
 Shard Router (determines which DB to query)
     ↓  ↓  ↓  ↓
 DB1  DB2  DB3  DB4  ← each is a full PostgreSQL server

SHARD KEY DECISION (most important choice):

 By user_id:
  - user_id 1-1M   → DB1
  - user_id 1M-2M  → DB2
  - Good: all user's data on one shard → no cross-shard queries for user context
  - Bad: hot users (power users) overwhelm one shard

 By geography:
  - EU users → DB-EU (physically in EU, GDPR compliance)
  - US users → DB-US
  - APAC     → DB-APAC
  - Good: data residency compliance, latency
  - Bad: cross-region reporting is complex

 By hash(user_id):
  - Distributes evenly, no hotspots
  - Bad: related data (user + orders) may be on different shards

CROSS-SHARD LIMITATIONS:
  - No JOIN across shards
  - No foreign keys across shards
  - No global ORDER BY with LIMIT (must merge sort in application)
  - No ACID transactions spanning shards (need Saga)
  - Re-sharding (splitting shards as you grow) is extremely painful
```

## Sharding in Spring Boot

```java
// Simple shard routing based on user_id:
@Configuration
public class ShardingConfig {

    private static final int SHARD_COUNT = 4;

    @Bean
    @Primary
    public DataSource routingDataSource(
            DataSource shard0, DataSource shard1,
            DataSource shard2, DataSource shard3) {
        Map<Object, Object> shards = Map.of(
            0, shard0, 1, shard1, 2, shard2, 3, shard3
        );

        AbstractRoutingDataSource routing = new AbstractRoutingDataSource() {
            @Override
            protected Object determineCurrentLookupKey() {
                Long userId = ShardContext.getCurrentUserId();
                return (int) (userId % SHARD_COUNT);
            }
        };
        routing.setTargetDataSources(shards);
        routing.setDefaultTargetDataSource(shard0);
        return routing;
    }
}

// Thread-local shard context:
public class ShardContext {
    private static final ThreadLocal<Long> USER_ID = new ThreadLocal<>();

    public static void setUserId(Long userId) { USER_ID.set(userId); }
    public static Long getCurrentUserId() { return USER_ID.get(); }
    public static void clear() { USER_ID.remove(); }
}

// Set shard context in request interceptor:
@Component
public class ShardInterceptor implements HandlerInterceptor {
    @Override
    public boolean preHandle(HttpServletRequest request,
                             HttpServletResponse response, Object handler) {
        Long userId = extractUserIdFromToken(request);
        ShardContext.setUserId(userId);
        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public void afterCompletion(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res,
                                Object handler, Exception ex) {
        ShardContext.clear();
    }
}
```

## When to Use Each

```
STEP 1: Vertical scaling (bigger server)      — up to ~128GB RAM, 64 cores
STEP 2: Add indexes + query optimization      — often 10-100× improvement
STEP 3: Caching (Redis)                       — reduce DB read load 80-90%
STEP 4: Read replicas                         — scale reads horizontally
STEP 5: Partitioning                          — manage large tables (100M+ rows)
STEP 6: Sharding                              — only when above are exhausted
                                               (usually 10TB+ of data or
                                                sustained millions of req/sec)

Most applications NEVER reach step 6.
Instagram ran on PostgreSQL with 1 billion users for years before sharding.
```

## Production Reality

Before recommending sharding in an interview (or at work), articulate why the simpler solutions aren't sufficient. Sharding introduces operational complexity that requires dedicated database expertise. If the question is about scale, a better first answer is often "we'd add a read replica for read scaling and archive old data to cold storage, partitioned by date" — this gets 80% of the benefit with 20% of the complexity.

