## Breaking vs Non-Breaking Changes

```
Non-breaking (safe — no version bump needed):
  + Adding new optional fields to response
  + Adding new optional request parameters
  + Adding new endpoints
  + Relaxing validation (accepting more values)

Breaking (requires new version):
  - Removing or renaming response fields
  - Changing field types (string → number)
  - Making previously optional field required
  - Changing HTTP status codes for existing operations
  - Removing endpoints
  - Changing URI structure

// Design principle: Postel's Law
// Be conservative in what you send, liberal in what you accept
// Accept old field names for backward compat:
public class CreateOrderRequest {
    @JsonProperty("userId")
    @JsonAlias({"user_id", "customerId"})  // Accept old names too
    private Long userId;
}
```

## Deprecation Strategy

```java
// Inform clients of deprecation via headers:
@GetMapping
@Deprecated
public ResponseEntity<List<OrderResponseV1>> listOrdersV1() {
    return ResponseEntity.ok()
        .header("Deprecation", "true")
        .header("Sunset", "Wed, 01 Jan 2025 00:00:00 GMT")  // When it goes away
        .header("Link", "</api/v2/orders>; rel=\"successor-version\"")
        .body(orderService.findAll().stream().map(v1Mapper::toResponse).collect(toList()));
}
// RFC 8594 defines Deprecation and Sunset headers
// Clients that handle these can warn developers to upgrade
```

## Production Reality

URI versioning with `/api/v1/` prefix is the practical choice for most APIs. Version at the resource level, not the operation level — all endpoints under `/api/v2/` share the same contract. Set a deprecation and sunset date before removing old versions, communicate it clearly in documentation and response headers, and stick to the date — API consumers plan migrations around your communicated timelines.

## How Auto-Configuration is Loaded

At startup, `AutoConfigurationImportSelector` reads the auto-configuration candidates from:
- **Spring Boot 3:** `META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports`
- **Spring Boot 2:** `META-INF/spring.factories` under key `EnableAutoConfiguration`

Each entry is a fully-qualified class name. Spring Boot loads all of them but only applies those whose conditions pass.

## Conditions That Control Activation

| Annotation | Activates when... |
|---|---|
| `@ConditionalOnClass` | a specific class is on the classpath |
| `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` | no bean of that type exists yet |
| `@ConditionalOnProperty` | a property matches a given value |
| `@ConditionalOnWebApplication` | running in a web context |

A typical auto-configuration class looks like:
```java
@AutoConfiguration
@ConditionalOnClass(DataSource.class)
@ConditionalOnMissingBean(DataSource.class)
public class DataSourceAutoConfiguration {
    @Bean
    public DataSource dataSource(DataSourceProperties props) {
        return props.initializeDataSourceBuilder().build();
    }
}
```

## Overriding Auto-Configuration

Define your own bean and auto-configuration steps aside:
```java
@Bean
public DataSource dataSource() {
    HikariDataSource ds = new HikariDataSource();
    ds.setMaximumPoolSize(50);
    return ds;
}
```

To disable an entire auto-configuration:
```java
@SpringBootApplication(exclude = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)
```

## Production Reality

The conditions evaluation report (`debug=true`) is your best debugging tool. When a bean isn't being created, the report tells you which condition failed — saves hours of guessing.

