## Context Caching

Spring caches the application context across test methods in the same JVM run. Tests with the same configuration share a context, making the test suite faster overall. Adding `@MockBean` or `@SpyBean` creates a new context — minimize their use for performance.

## Production Reality

A test suite with 500 `@SpringBootTest` tests is painful. Structure your tests in a pyramid: many fast unit tests, some `@WebMvcTest` / `@DataJpaTest` slice tests, few `@SpringBootTest` integration tests. The full-context tests should cover the wiring and end-to-end happy paths — not every business logic variation.

questions.codeExample:
Spring AOP provides five advice types. `@Around` is the most powerful because it wraps the method call entirely, but using it when `@Before` would suffice adds unnecessary complexity and potential for bugs (forgetting to call `proceed()`).

```java
@Aspect
@Component
public class LoggingAspect {
    @Before("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
    public void logEntry(JoinPoint jp) {
        log.debug("Entering {}", jp.getSignature().getName());
    }

    @AfterReturning(pointcut = "execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))",
                    returning = "result")
    public void logSuccess(JoinPoint jp, Object result) {
        log.debug("{} returned {}", jp.getSignature().getName(), result);
    }

    @AfterThrowing(pointcut = "execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))",
                   throwing = "ex")
    public void logFailure(JoinPoint jp, Exception ex) {
        log.error("{} threw {}", jp.getSignature().getName(), ex.getMessage());
    }

    @After("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
    public void logExit(JoinPoint jp) {
        log.debug("Exiting {}", jp.getSignature().getName());
    }
}
```

**`@Before`** runs before the method — use for validation, logging entry points, security checks. **`@AfterReturning`** runs only on successful return — use for audit logging or result transformation. **`@AfterThrowing`** runs only when an exception propagates — use for error monitoring. **`@After`** runs regardless of outcome (like `finally`) — use for cleanup. **`@Around`** wraps the entire call and controls whether the original method executes at all — use for timing, transactions, caching, circuit breakers.

The execution order when all five apply: `@Around` (pre-proceed) → `@Before` → method executes → `@AfterReturning` or `@AfterThrowing` → `@After` → `@Around` (post-proceed).

The last objection to constructor injection was always boilerplate — a service with six dependencies means a six-parameter constructor with six field assignments. Lombok's `@RequiredArgsConstructor` eliminates this entirely, and it's now the dominant pattern in production Spring Boot codebases.

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class OrderService {
    private final UserRepository userRepository;
    private final PaymentGateway paymentGateway;
    private final InventoryService inventoryService;
    private final NotificationService notificationService;
}
```

Lombok generates the constructor at compile time. Since there's only one constructor, Spring injects without needing `@Autowired`. The test writes itself naturally — no Spring context, no reflection, no `@InjectMocks` magic.

```java
class OrderServiceTest {
    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        service = new OrderService(
            mock(UserRepository.class),
            mock(PaymentGateway.class),
            mock(InventoryService.class),
            mock(NotificationService.class)
        );
    }
}
```

If someone adds a dependency to `OrderService`, this test fails to compile — forcing the test to be updated. With field injection, the new dependency would silently be `null`, causing a NullPointerException somewhere deeper in the test run. One signal that a class has too many dependencies: when the constructor exceeds five or six parameters, it's not a constructor problem — it's a **Single Responsibility Problem**. The constructor is telling you the class does too much.

