```java
@Component
public class JwtUtil {

    @Value("${jwt.secret}")
    private String secret;  // At least 256 bits / 32 chars for HS256

    @Value("${jwt.expiration-ms}")
    private long expirationMs;  // e.g., 900000 = 15 minutes

    private SecretKey getSigningKey() {
        return Keys.hmacShaKeyFor(Decoders.BASE64.decode(secret));
    }

    // Generate JWT
    public String generateToken(UserDetails userDetails) {
        Map<String, Object> claims = new HashMap<>();
        claims.put("roles", userDetails.getAuthorities().stream()
            .map(GrantedAuthority::getAuthority)
            .collect(Collectors.toList()));

        return Jwts.builder()
            .claims(claims)
            .subject(userDetails.getUsername())
            .issuedAt(new Date())
            .expiration(new Date(System.currentTimeMillis() + expirationMs))
            .signWith(getSigningKey())
            .compact();
    }

    // Extract username from token
    public String extractUsername(String token) {
        return extractClaim(token, Claims::getSubject);
    }

    // Extract any claim
    public <T> T extractClaim(String token, Function<Claims, T> claimsResolver) {
        final Claims claims = extractAllClaims(token);
        return claimsResolver.apply(claims);
    }

    private Claims extractAllClaims(String token) {
        return Jwts.parser()
            .verifyWith(getSigningKey())
            .build()
            .parseSignedClaims(token)
            .getPayload();
        // Throws: JwtException if signature invalid, ExpiredJwtException if expired
    }

    // Validate token
    public boolean isTokenValid(String token, UserDetails userDetails) {
        try {
            final String username = extractUsername(token);
            return username.equals(userDetails.getUsername()) && !isTokenExpired(token);
        } catch (JwtException e) {
            return false;  // Invalid signature, malformed, etc.
        }
    }

    private boolean isTokenExpired(String token) {
        return extractClaim(token, Claims::getExpiration).before(new Date());
    }
}
```

## HMAC vs RSA Signing

```java
// HMAC (HS256) — symmetric: same key for sign and verify
// Good for: monolith or single service
// Risk: any service that verifies can also forge tokens

// RSA (RS256) — asymmetric
// Private key: signs tokens (auth service only)
// Public key: verifies tokens (all other services can verify, can't forge)

// RSA in JwtUtil:
private PrivateKey privateKey;  // Loaded from keystore
private PublicKey publicKey;

String token = Jwts.builder()
    .subject(username)
    .signWith(privateKey, Jwts.SIG.RS256)
    .compact();

Claims claims = Jwts.parser()
    .verifyWith(publicKey)  // Any service can have the public key
    .build()
    .parseSignedClaims(token)
    .getPayload();
```

## Production Reality

Store the JWT secret in a secrets manager (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault) — not in application.properties files that get committed to Git. Rotate keys periodically. For microservices, use RSA: the auth service holds the private key, other services get the public key via a JWKS endpoint (`/.well-known/jwks.json`) and verify tokens without contacting the auth service.

## Optimistic Locking with JPA @Version

```java
@Entity
public class Product {
    @Id
    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private int stockQuantity;

    @Version  // JPA manages this automatically
    private Long version;  // incremented on every update
}

// Repository layer — no explicit lock needed
@Transactional
public void decrementStock(Long productId) {
    Product p = repo.findById(productId).orElseThrow();
    if (p.getStockQuantity() < 1) throw new OutOfStockException();
    p.setStockQuantity(p.getStockQuantity() - 1);
    // JPA generates: UPDATE product SET stock=?, version=6 WHERE id=1 AND version=5
    // If another tx updated it (version is now 6), this UPDATE matches 0 rows
    // → JPA throws OptimisticLockException
}
```

## Pessimistic Locking with JPA @Lock

```java
@Repository
public interface ProductRepository extends JpaRepository<Product, Long> {
    @Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)  // SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
    @Query("SELECT p FROM Product p WHERE p.id = :id")
    Optional<Product> findByIdForUpdate(@Param("id") Long id);
}

@Transactional
public void processOrder(Long productId) {
    // Lock acquired here; other transactions block until this commits
    Product p = repo.findByIdForUpdate(productId).orElseThrow();
    p.setStockQuantity(p.getStockQuantity() - 1);
    // Lock released when @Transactional method ends
}
```

## Decision Guide

| Scenario | Locking Strategy |
|----------|-----------------|
| Low contention (user editing own profile) | Optimistic |
| High contention (seat reservation, inventory) | Pessimistic |
| Long read-then-write workflows | Optimistic with retry |
| Financial transactions | Pessimistic |

## Resource-Oriented URL Design

```
RESOURCE DESIGN RULES:

1. Nouns for resources, not verbs:
   WRONG: GET  /api/getOrders
          POST /api/createOrder
          POST /api/deleteOrder?id=42
   RIGHT: GET  /api/orders
          POST /api/orders
          DELETE /api/orders/42

2. Hierarchical relationships:
   /api/orders                        All orders
   /api/orders/42                     Specific order
   /api/orders/42/items               Items in that order
   /api/orders/42/items/5             Specific item in that order

3. Keep hierarchy shallow (max 2-3 levels):
   /api/users/7/orders/42/items/5/reviews  ← Too deep
   Instead: /api/order-items/5/reviews    ← Flatten with compound resource

4. Actions that don't map to CRUD:
   POST /api/orders/42/cancel         ← Use sub-resource for actions
   POST /api/orders/42/refund
   POST /api/payments/42/retry
   (not POST /api/cancelOrder?id=42)
```

## HTTP Status Codes — Use Correctly

```
2xx SUCCESS:
  200 OK           — Successful GET, PATCH, DELETE with body
  201 Created      — Successful POST that created a resource
                     Include Location header: Location: /api/orders/42
  202 Accepted     — Async operation started (not yet complete)
  204 No Content   — Successful DELETE or action with no response body

4xx CLIENT ERRORS:
  400 Bad Request  — Invalid request body, missing required field
  401 Unauthorized — Not authenticated (no/invalid token)
  403 Forbidden    — Authenticated but no permission
  404 Not Found    — Resource doesn't exist
  409 Conflict     — Duplicate resource, optimistic lock conflict
  422 Unprocessable Entity — Validation failed (valid JSON, invalid business logic)
  429 Too Many Requests    — Rate limited

5xx SERVER ERRORS:
  500 Internal Server Error — Unexpected error (never expose stack traces!)
  502 Bad Gateway          — Upstream service error
  503 Service Unavailable  — Circuit breaker open, maintenance mode
  504 Gateway Timeout      — Upstream timed out

COMMON MISTAKES:
  ✗ Return 200 with {"error": true} body → breaks HTTP semantics
  ✗ Return 500 for bad user input → should be 400
  ✗ Return 404 for auth errors → exposes resource existence; use 403
```

