## Active-Active vs Active-Passive

```
ACTIVE-PASSIVE (Failover):
  Region A (primary, handling traffic)
  Region B (standby, replicating data, not serving traffic)

  If Region A fails:
    → DNS failover to Region B (can take 1-5 minutes)
    → Region B starts serving traffic
    → During failover: downtime (from seconds to minutes depending on setup)

  Good for: simpler to implement and reason about
  Downtime during failover: yes (RPO/RTO consideration)

ACTIVE-ACTIVE:
  Region A handles US traffic (50%)
  Region B handles EU traffic (50%)

  If Region A fails:
    → DNS routes all traffic to Region B immediately
    → No downtime (already serving traffic)
    → Region B must handle double load (provision for this)

  Harder: data replication must be bidirectional and conflict-free
  Read-only active-active: easy (replicas in each region, writes to single primary)
  Read-write active-active: hard (need CRDTs or careful conflict resolution)

FOR MOST TEAMS: Active-passive across AZs (within same region) is sufficient.
  - All within same AWS region (us-east-1)
  - Services spread across 3 AZs (availability zones)
  - If AZ goes down: other 2 continue serving, K8s restarts pods elsewhere
  - This gives ~99.99% availability with manageable complexity
```

## Graceful Degradation

```java
@Service
public class ProductPageService {

    private final ProductRepository productRepo;
    private final RecommendationService recommendationService;
    private final ReviewService reviewService;

    // Recommendations are non-critical — degrade gracefully if they fail:
    public ProductPageData getProductPage(Long productId) {
        Product product = productRepo.findById(productId).orElseThrow();

        // Fetch recommendations with circuit breaker fallback:
        List<Product> recommendations;
        try {
            recommendations = recommendationService.getRelated(productId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.warn("Recommendations unavailable for product {}, using fallback", productId);
            recommendations = productRepo.findTopSellers(10);  // Generic fallback
        }

        // Fetch reviews with timeout fallback:
        Optional<ReviewSummary> reviews;
        try {
            reviews = reviewService.getSummary(productId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.warn("Reviews unavailable for product {}", productId);
            reviews = Optional.empty();  // Page renders without reviews
        }

        return ProductPageData.builder()
            .product(product)
            .recommendations(recommendations)  // Generic OR personalized
            .reviews(reviews.orElse(null))     // Null → UI shows 'Reviews loading...'
            .build();
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

For an intermediate developer, knowing the Kubernetes liveness/readiness probe distinction is high-value: liveness failure restarts the pod (fixes crashes), readiness failure removes it from the load balancer (handles temporary overload or dependency failure). Getting this wrong causes cascading restart loops in production. Also: graceful shutdown is part of HA — configure `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` and handle `SIGTERM` in your application to allow in-flight requests to complete before the pod stops.

## Making Services Stateless — Prerequisite for Horizontal Scaling

```java
// STATEFUL (BAD for horizontal scaling):
@RestController
class OrderController {
    private Map<String, User> sessionStore = new HashMap<>();  // In-memory!

    @PostMapping("/login")
    public String login(@RequestBody LoginRequest req) {
        User user = authenticate(req);
        String sessionId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        sessionStore.put(sessionId, user);  // Stored in Instance A's memory
        return sessionId;
    }

    @GetMapping("/orders")
    public List<Order> getOrders(@RequestHeader("Session-Id") String sessionId) {
        User user = sessionStore.get(sessionId);  // Only works if Instance A!
        // If load balancer routes this request to Instance B → user not found
    }
}

// STATELESS (GOOD — any instance can handle any request):
@RestController
class OrderController {
    private final RedisTemplate<String, User> redis;  // Shared Redis

    @PostMapping("/login")
    public String login(@RequestBody LoginRequest req) {
        User user = authenticate(req);
        String sessionId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        redis.opsForValue().set("session:" + sessionId, user, Duration.ofHours(8));
        return sessionId;
        // Session stored in Redis — any instance can find it
    }

    @GetMapping("/orders")
    public List<Order> getOrders(@RequestHeader("Session-Id") String sessionId) {
        User user = redis.opsForValue().get("session:" + sessionId);
        // Works on any instance — all read from same Redis
    }
}
```

## Load Balancing Algorithms

```
ROUND ROBIN (default):
  Request 1 → Instance A
  Request 2 → Instance B
  Request 3 → Instance C
  Request 4 → Instance A (repeat)

  Good for: homogeneous requests with similar processing time
  Bad for: mixed load (some requests take 1ms, others take 5s)

LEAST CONNECTIONS:
  Route to instance with fewest active connections
  Good for: varying request duration
  Nginx: upstream { least_conn; server a; server b; server c; }

IP HASH (sticky sessions):
  hash(client_ip) % num_instances → always routes same IP to same server
  Good for: stateful services (shouldn't exist!), WebSocket connections
  Bad for: uneven distribution if many clients share an IP (NAT)

WEIGHTED:
  Instance A: weight=3 (newer, more powerful)
  Instance B: weight=1 (older)
  → 3:1 traffic ratio
  Good for: rolling upgrades, mixed server capacities
```

## Database Read Scaling with Replicas

```
ARCHITECTURE:
  All WRITES → Primary PostgreSQL
       ↓ (replication: sync or async)
  READS → Replica 1
          Replica 2
          Replica 3

JAVA ROUTING (AbstractRoutingDataSource):
```

```java
@Configuration
public class DataSourceConfig {

    @Bean
    public DataSource routingDataSource(
            @Qualifier("primaryDataSource") DataSource primary,
            @Qualifier("replicaDataSource") DataSource replica) {

        AbstractRoutingDataSource routing = new AbstractRoutingDataSource() {
            @Override
            protected Object determineCurrentLookupKey() {
                return TransactionSynchronizationManager.isCurrentTransactionReadOnly()
                    ? "replica" : "primary";
            }
        };
        routing.setTargetDataSources(Map.of("primary", primary, "replica", replica));
        routing.setDefaultTargetDataSource(primary);
        return routing;
    }
}

// Service usage:
@Transactional(readOnly = true)   // Routes to replica
public List<Order> getOrderHistory(Long userId) { ... }

@Transactional                     // Routes to primary (readOnly=false default)
public Order createOrder(CreateOrderRequest req) { ... }
