  min-height: 100vh;
}
.header  { grid-area: header; }   /* placement by NAME */
.sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; }

/* FLEXBOX — the layout is a DISTRIBUTION along one axis */
.toolbar {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;   /* logo left, actions right */
  align-items: center;
  gap: 12px;                          /* spacing, no margins */
}
```

The three-question test (the interview answer):
1. Do rows and columns need to ALIGN with each other? → Grid. (Flexbox wraps independently — line 2 doesn't know line 1's column positions.)
2. Does the CONTAINER define the structure, or does the CONTENT? Container-defined → Grid; content-driven → Flex.
3. Do you need to place items at specific coordinates / overlap them? → Grid only (grid-area placement, z-index layering, item spanning 2 columns while its neighbor spans 3 rows).

The composition pattern (what real codebases look like):
```
Grid: page shell (sidebar / main / header / footer — 2D)
  └─ Flex: navbar (logo left, menu right — 1D)
  └─ Grid: card grid inside main (3-column, aligned — 2D)
       └─ Flex: each card's title/meta/actions (1D)
```

The gap property note: both support gap now — the margin-collision/spacing-cleanup win that used to need lobotomized owl selectors.

The two-axis model (the key mental shift), verified conceptually:

```css
/* The container — one axis is main, the other is cross */
.navbar {
  display: flex;            /* children become flex items */
  flex-direction: row;       /* main axis: horizontal (default) */
  justify-content: space-between;  /* main-axis: logo left, links right */
  align-items: center;       /* cross-axis: vertically centered */
  gap: 16px;                 /* spacing between items — no margin hacks */
}

/* The children — the flex trio */
.sidebar { flex: 0 0 260px; }   /* fixed: don't grow, don't shrink, 260px */
.content { flex: 1; }            /* 1 1 0: absorbs ALL remaining space */

/* The famous centering — three declarations */
.center-everything {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;   /* main axis */
  align-items: center;        /* cross axis */
}
```

The flex shorthand decoded (the interview core):
- flex: 1 → 1 1 0%: grow 1 share, shrinkable, basis 0 → equal-width columns regardless of content
- flex: auto → 1 1 auto: grows AND sizes from its content first
- flex: none → 0 0 auto: refuses to grow or shrink (fixed)
- flex-grow 2 vs 1: two items with grow 2 and 1 split free space 2:1 — grow is a RATIO, not a size

When Flexbox vs Grid (the decision question): Flexbox = one axis, content-driven, 'distribute these along a line' (it wraps but each line is independent). Grid = two axes, container-driven, 'place these in a coordinate system.' The navbar/toolbar/split-pane is flex; the page layout/dashboard/magazine-grid is Grid. They compose: a Grid page area containing a flex toolbar.

The mobile-first structure, verified conceptually:

```css
/* BASE = mobile (no query — the smallest screen IS the default) */
.cards { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
.card  { padding: 1rem; }

/* Layer complexity UP as space appears (min-width, not max-width) */
@media (min-width: 48em) {          /* tablet */
  .cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
}
@media (min-width: 75em) {          /* desktop */
  .cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  .card  { padding: 2rem; }
}

/* The query-free alternative — grid decides by space available */
.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
  /* columns = however many fit ≥16rem; no breakpoints needed */
}

/* Fluid type — one rule, all screens */
h1 { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1rem + 2vw, 3rem); }  /* min, preferred, max */
```

Why mobile-first min-width (the reasoning interviewers want):
- Performance: phones download only the base; desktop adds layers — the constrained device gets the least CSS to process
- Constraint breeds clarity: designing small forces content prioritization; adding is easier than subtracting
- Fewer overrides: max-width desktop-first needs 'undo' rules as screens shrink; min-width only ADDS — no overridden properties

Choosing breakpoints (the discipline that separates juniors): resize the window and watch WHERE the design breaks — set breakpoints there, at content boundaries. Copying Bootstrap's 576/768/992/1200 verbatim for a layout that breaks at 900px is the anti-pattern.

The model and the trap, verified by arithmetic:

```css
/* content-box (default) — the width you say is NOT the width you see */
.box { width: 200px; padding: 20px; border: 5px; box-sizing: content-box; }
/* rendered width = 200 + 20×2 + 5×2 = 250px  (arithmetic, not opinion) */

/* border-box — what you say is what you see */
.box { width: 200px; padding: 20px; border: 5px; box-sizing: border-box; }
/* rendered width = 200px — padding and border subtract from content */

/* the universal fix — the first line of most modern stylesheets */
*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
```

The four layers, inside-out (the interview structure):
- Content: the text/media area; its size = width/height (or the content's natural size)
- Padding: clears space inside the border; takes the element's background (padding shows the background color/image)
- Border: the drawn edge; width, style, color; padding ends where border begins
- Margin: the invisible force field OUTSIDE; transparent; pushes OTHER elements; collapses with adjacent margins

The two facts that show real understanding:
1. Margin collapse (the classic gotcha): adjacent vertical margins collapse to the LARGER of the two — two stacked divs with margin: 20px have 20px between them, not 40. Padding never collapses. Flex/grid containers disable collapse in their children.
2. background coverage: background paints content + padding, NOT margin — a margin shows whatever's behind the element.

The mental model first (what most candidates miss): CORS is the BROWSER enforcing a SERVER's policy. Your fetch fired, the server responded — the browser REFUSED to hand the response to your JS because the server didn't declare your origin allowed. The console error is the browser's enforcement, not a network failure.

```js
// THE MECHANICS
// same origin = scheme + host + port all match
// http://app.com  vs  https://app.com  → different (scheme)
// http://app.com  vs  http://api.app.com → different (host)
// http://app.com:3000 vs http://app.com:8080 → different (port)

// SIMPLE requests (GET, POST+form types, no custom headers) — sent directly,
// browser checks the response header:
fetch('https://api.example.com/data');
// response must include: Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://app.com (or *)
// missing/mismatched → browser blocks YOUR READING of it. Console: CORS error.

// PREFLIGHTED requests (PUT/DELETE, custom headers like Authorization,
// JSON Content-Type) — browser sends OPTIONS first:
// OPTIONS https://api.example.com/data
// server must answer:
//   Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://app.com
//   Access-Control-Allow-Methods: PUT, DELETE
//   Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization
//   (Access-Control-Max-Age: 86400 — cache the preflight, stop re-asking)
// OPTIONS rejected → the real request never even fires.
```

The fixes (in order of correctness):
1. Server sends the right headers — THE fix. Express: `cors({origin: 'http://app.com'})`; a list of allowed origins; or `*` for a genuinely public API.
2. Credentialed requests (cookies) need MORE: `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` AND a SPECIFIC origin — `*` is forbidden with credentials (the browser rejects the combination; a common trap).
3. Same-origin the deployment: serve frontend and API from one origin (or subdomain with proper headers) — production apps often avoid CORS entirely by path (reverse proxy: /api → backend).
4. Dev-only: the Vite/webpack dev-server proxy — your code calls /api (same origin), the dev server forwards to the backend. This is NOT a production fix; it's a dev ergonomics tool that also prevents CORS noise in dev.

What does NOT work (the honesty test): JSONP (dead), 'disabling CORS in the browser' (a dev-machine flag, tests nothing real), and no-cors mode fetch (you can SEND cross-origin simple requests but never READ them — useless for APIs).

The two scenarios and their debug paths:

```jsx
// SCENARIO A: infinite re-render — the classic self-feeding effect
useEffect(() => {
  setItems(createItems());   // writes state on EVERY run...
}, []);                      // ...but if the dep array is WRONG:
// useEffect(() => { setItems(...) }, [items])  ← items changes → effect
//   runs → setItems → new items → effect runs → INFINITE LOOP

// The fix: deps must list what the effect READS, and the effect
// must not write that same value. Derive instead of sync:
const derived = useMemo(() => createItems(dep), [dep]);  // no state

// SCENARIO B: stale list data — mutation killed the re-render
const [todos, setTodos] = useState([]);
const buggy = () => { todos.push(newItem); setTodos(todos); };
//        ↑ same array reference — React compares Object.is,
//          sees 'no change', skips re-render. UI stale.
const fixed = () => setTodos([...todos, newItem]);   // new ref ✓
```

The systematic process (what the interviewer is really testing):
1. Reproduce reliably — find the exact action that triggers it
2. Localize — bisect: comment half the component, does it still happen? which state update?
3. Read the data flow — for each piece of state: who initializes, who writes, who reads, and is the write's reference NEW?
4. Check the React-specific suspects: (a) effect writing state it depends on, (b) object/array mutation, (c) parent re-rendering with unstable props (inline object/function props to a memo'd child), (d) missing/wrong key in a list making items mismatch across renders
5. Verify with React DevTools Profiler — it names the exact commit's cause ('hook changed', 'props changed') instead of guessing
6. Fix root cause + add the lint rule that would have caught it (exhaustive-deps)

The patterns, verified:

```js
// Object — by key, rename, default, rest
const { a, b: renamed, c = 5, ...restObj } = { a: 1, b: 2, x: 9, y: 10 };
// a=1, renamed=2, c=5 (default — key absent), restObj={x:9,y:10} ✓

// Array — by position, skip, rest
const [first, , third, ...restArr] = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];
// first=10, third=30 (20 SKIPPED), restArr=[40,50] ✓

// Swap without a temp — the classic
let p = 1, q = 2; [p, q] = [q, p];   // p=2, q=1 ✓

// Nested — drill into structure
const { name, address: { city } } = { name: 'Al', address: { city: 'Blr' } };
// name='Al', city='Blr' ✓

// Function params — the options-object idiom (production pattern)
function draw({ size = 10, color = 'red' } = {}) { return `${color} ${size}`; }
draw();            // 'red 10' — both defaults fire ✓
draw({ size: 20 }); // 'red 20' — only size overrides ✓
```

Why it matters (the interview core):
- Named parameters from an options object: callers see WHAT each argument is (`draw({color, size})` beats `draw(color, size)`), order-independent, extensible without breaking call sites
- Selective extraction: pull 2 fields from a 20-field API response in one line
- React/hooks idiom: `const { useState, useEffect } = React;` — the ecosystem runs on destructuring

The failure modes worth naming: destructuring a property that's undefined THROWS (`const { a: { b } } = {}` → TypeError: cannot destructure undefined) — guard with defaults at each level (`= {}`); and array destructuring an object / object destructuring an array silently misbehaves rather than erroring.

The basics, verified:

```js
async function loadUser(id) {
  // await pauses HERE (the async function, not the thread)
  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  const user = await response.json();
  return user;  // the async function returns a Promise wrapping user
}

// It IS a Promise:
loadUser(1).then(user => console.log(user));
// or: const user = await loadUser(1); — in another async fn
```

The two keywords:
- async function: always returns a Promise — even returning a plain value wraps it in a resolved Promise
- await: can only be used inside async (or top-level modules) — suspends the async function until the Promise settles, then resumes with the resolved value (or throws on rejection)

The translation to Promise form (what async/await ACTUALLY is):
```js
async function loadUser(id) { ... }
// is equivalent to:
function loadUser(id) {
  return fetch(`/api/users/${id}`)
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(user => user)  // same chain, sequential syntax
}
```

The execution model (the misconception to correct): await does NOT block the thread. It SUSPENDS the async function (the rest of the function becomes the .then callback), and the event loop continues — other code, other Promises, the UI all keep running. When the awaited Promise resolves, the async function resumes.

The error handling (the pattern difference):
```js
// Promise form
fetch(url).then(d => ...).catch(e => ...)

// async/await form — synchronous-looking try/catch
try {
  const data = await fetch(url);
} catch (e) {
  // the rejection is catchable
}
```

The sequential-vs-parallel trap (the #1 async/await interview question):
```js
// SEQUENTIAL (wrong for independent calls — each waits for the previous):
const a = await fetchA();  // 1s
const b = await fetchB();  // 1s more — 2s total

// PARALLEL (correct for independent calls — both start at once):
const [a, b] = await Promise.all([fetchA(), fetchB()]);  // 1s total
```

The correct patterns, verified:

```js
// POST with JSON — the three musts
const res = await fetch('/api/users', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },  // must set explicitly
  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Al' }),                // must stringify
});

// THE trap: 404/500 do NOT throw
const res = await fetch('/api/missing');
res.ok;        // false (status 404)
res.status;    // 404
// fetch did NOT reject — you must check:
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);

// Cancellation — AbortController
const ctrl = new AbortController();
const req = fetch('/api/slow', { signal: ctrl.signal });
ctrl.abort();  // req rejects with AbortError ✓ (verified)
// the production pattern: abort on unmount / new search / timeout

// Response body — a one-shot stream
const data = await res.json();   // consumes the stream
// await res.json() again → TypeError: body already read
```

The three-layer error model (the interview structure):
1. Network/refused/CORS/aborted → fetch itself rejects → .catch
2. HTTP error status (404, 500) → fetch RESOLVES → check res.ok / res.status
3. Body parse issues (invalid JSON) → res.json() rejects

Production wrapper (what a complete answer looks like):
```js
async function api(path, opts) {
  const res = await fetch(path, opts);
  if (!res.ok) {
    const err = new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    err.status = res.status;
    try { err.body = await res.json(); } catch {}
    throw err;
  }
  return res.json();
}
```

When to reach for a library: React Query/SWR add caching, dedupe, retry, background refresh — the things fetch leaves you to build.

The mechanics, verified:

```js
// Chaining — each .then gets the previous result
Promise.resolve(1)
  .then(v => v + 1)      // 2
  .then(v => v * 10)     // 20
  .then(v => console.log(v));  // chain: 20 ✓

// Each .then returns a NEW promise (not the same one)
const p2 = Promise.resolve('a');
const t1 = p2.then(() => 'b');
// t1 !== p2 — true (verified). Chains are pipelines, not broadcasts.

// Errors skip fulfilled handlers, propagate to nearest .catch
Promise.reject(new Error('boom'))
  .then(() => console.log('skipped'))     // skipped
  .catch(e => console.log(e.message))      // 'boom' ✓
  .then(() => 'catch recovers — chain continues');  // ✓

// .finally runs regardless, passes the value through
Promise.resolve(42).finally(cleanup).then(v => v);  // still 42 ✓
```

The state machine (the interview core):
- pending → fulfilled (via resolve) or pending → rejected (via reject)
- Settled is permanent: resolve-then-reject does nothing; a fulfilled promise never re-fires .then
- Handlers attach anytime: before or after settlement — late subscribers to an already-settled promise fire as microtasks immediately

The chaining rules that follow:
- Returning a value in .then → next .then receives it
- Returning a PROMISE in .then → the chain waits for it, then unwraps (the flat-map behavior — no .then nesting)
- Throwing in .then → the chain rejects at that point, skipping to the next .catch

The constructor (where promises are born):
```js
new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  // the executor runs SYNCHRONOUSLY
  doAsync((err, data) => err ? reject(err) : resolve(data));
});
```

The differences, verified:

```js
// THIS — the headline difference
const obj = {
  name: 'Outer',
  regular() { return this.name; },   // this = the receiver (obj) → 'Outer' ✓
  arrow: () => this?.name,            // this = enclosing scope → undefined/global ✓
  delay() {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(this.name), 100);  // arrow INSIDE method:
  }                                    // captures the method's this → 'Outer' ✓
};
obj.regular();   // 'Outer'
obj.arrow();     // undefined (verified — no own this)
obj.delay();     // logs 'Outer' — the arrow kept the method's this
```

The mental model (the interview core): a regular function's `this` is decided AT CALL TIME — who called it (obj.method() → obj; plain call → undefined/global; new → the new instance). An arrow's `this` is decided AT WRITE TIME — it's the enclosing scope's this, permanently, exactly like a closed-over variable. That's why the setTimeout-in-a-method pattern works: the arrow inherits the method's this instead of losing it to the timer's call context.

Where each belongs (the practical answer):
- Arrow: callbacks that must KEEP the surrounding this (event handlers in class methods, timers, .map/.filter/.then continuations), and short pure transforms `x => x * 2`
- Regular: object methods (need dynamic this for chaining/receivers), anything used with new, functions needing arguments, and when you want hoisting (function declarations hoist; const arrows don't — TDZ)

The rest of the checklist: no arguments object in arrows (use `(...args) =>`), no prototype property, can't be called with new, implicit return only for a single expression (a block body needs explicit return).

The semantics, verified by the module system in use:

```js
// math.js — named exports + one default per module
export const PI = 3.14159;
export function square(x) { return x * x; }
export default class Calculator {}   // at most ONE default

// app.js
import Calc, { PI, square } from './math.js';  // default first, no braces
import * as math from './math.js';               // namespace import
import { PI as TAU } from './math.js';            // rename on import
// ESM is static: import paths are strings, resolved and linked BEFORE execution
```

The ESM vs CommonJS table (the interview core):

```
                ESM                     CommonJS
syntax          import / export          require / module.exports
resolution      STATIC (parse-time)      DYNAMIC (runtime, any expression)
loading         async, parallel         sync, blocking
value binding   LIVE view of export     COPY at require-time
this at top     undefined               exports/{} object
files           .mjs (or type:module)   .cjs (default in Node)
hoisting        imports hoisted         require runs in place
```

The live-binding difference (the follow-up that tests depth):
```js
// ESM: counter.js
export let count = 0;
export function inc() { count++; }
// importer sees count CHANGE as inc() is called — the import IS the binding

// CommonJS: counter.cjs
let count = 0; module.exports = { count, inc };  // count COPIED at require
// importer's count stays 0 forever — it got a snapshot
```

Why browsers/Node both moved to ESM (the ecosystem answer): static imports enable tree-shaking (bundlers PROVE which exports are unused and delete them), async loading enables parallel fetch of the module graph, and one module format across browser/Node/tools replaced the UMD/AMD era. package.json type: module flips a project to ESM; the practical rule: new code is ESM, CommonJS interop via import cjs from './legacy.cjs' works (the default export = module.exports).

The four, verified:

```js
// Promise.all — fail-fast, order preserved
const r = await Promise.all([p(1), p(2), p(3)]);  // [1, 2, 3] ✓
// if p(2) rejects → all rejects immediately with that error,
// and p(1), p(3) results are DISCARDED (they still run)

// allSettled — never rejects; you inspect each outcome
const s = await Promise.allSettled([p(1), fail('x'), p(3)]);
// [{status:'fulfilled',value:1}, {status:'rejected',reason:x}, {status:'fulfilled',value:3}] ✓

// race — first to settle wins, whatever it is
Promise.race([slow(50ms), p('fast')]);  // 'fast' ✓
// the timeout pattern: race([fetch(), timeoutPromise]) — timeout rejects → you bail

// any — first FULFILLMENT wins; rejections ignored
// Promise.any([fail(a), p('B'), p('C')])  → 'B'
// Promise.any([fail(a), fail(b)]) → rejects AggregateError with all errors
```

The decision table (the interview answer):
- Every result required or fail together → all (API responses composing one screen)
- Partial failures acceptable, want the survivors → allSettled (batch uploads, analytics beacons)
- First result, or a timeout/cancel signal → race (fetch with deadline)
- First SUCCESS from redundant sources; all-fail is the only failure → any (multi-CDN, multi-region lookup)

The two traps worth naming: (1) with all, the non-failed promises KEEP RUNNING after one fails — all doesn't cancel anything, it just stops waiting; (2) all preserves input order in its output regardless of completion order — the results array maps to the input array, not the timing.

The two directions, verified:

```js
// SPREAD — one collection → many elements
const arr1 = [1, 2];
const withMore = [...arr1, 3];            // [1,2,3] ✓
const mergedObj = { ...{a: 1}, b: 2 };    // {a:1, b:2} ✓
console.log(Math.max(...[3, 1, 2]));       // 3 — array → args ✓
const cloned = [...arr1];                   // new array (shallow) ✓

// REST — many elements → one collection
function sum(...items) {                   // gathers ALL args
  return items.reduce((s, n) => s + n, 0); // items is a REAL array
}
sum(1, 2, 3, 4);                           // 10 ✓
const [head, ...tail] = [1, 2, 3, 4];     // head=1, tail=[2,3,4]

// The distinction in one line each:
// f(...arr)  — spread: I HAVE an array, you WANT separate args
// f(...rest) — rest:   you SEND separate args, I WANT an array
```

The replacement uses (what spread eliminated from older code):
- Copy: `[...arr]` replaced `arr.slice()` / `arr.concat()`
- Merge objects: `{...a, ...b}` replaced `Object.assign({}, a, b)` — note right-wins on key conflicts (b's values overwrite a's), the behavior you rely on for overrides: `{...defaults, ...userConfig}`
- Array-to-args: `Math.max(...nums)` replaced `Math.max.apply(null, nums)`

The rest-vs-arguments modernization (the follow-up): `function f() { arguments }` gives an array-LIKE object — indexed and has length, but NO map/filter/reduce, and it's absent in arrow functions. Rest params are a real array, work in arrows, and can be positioned after named params: `function f(prefix, ...rest)`.

The mechanism, verified:

```js
// THE CHAIN — property access walks up until found or null
const animal = { eat() { return 'eating'; } };
const dog = Object.create(animal);         // dog's [[Prototype]] = animal
dog.eat();        // 'eating' — found on animal via the chain ✓
dog.hasOwnProperty('eat');   // false — it's on the PROTOTYPE ✓
dog.bark = () => 'woof';      // own property — shadows nothing

// THE CONSTRUCTOR PATTERN (what pre-class code looked like)
function Cat(name) { this.name = name; }       // per-INSTANCE data
Cat.prototype.meow = function() { return this.name + ' meows'; };  // SHARED method
const c = new Cat('Mia');
c.meow();                        // 'Mia meows' — method found on Cat.prototype ✓
Object.getPrototypeOf(c) === Cat.prototype;   // true ✓
// chain: c → Cat.prototype → Object.prototype → null

// CLASS = THE SAME THING, SWEETER
// CLASS = THE SAME THING, SWEETER
class Rabbit {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }   // own data per instance
  hop() { return this.name + ' hops'; }     // → Rabbit.prototype.hop ✓
}
const r = new Rabbit('Bun');
r.hasOwnProperty('hop');  // false — prototype method ✓ (verified)
```

Why methods live on the prototype (the design question):
- ONE function object shared by all instances — memory: 1000 instances, 1 meow
- vs. putting methods in the constructor (this.meow = ...) — 1000 copies
- This is why hasOwnProperty('hop') is false: the method belongs to the CLASS, not the instance — and it's why inheritance is CHEAP: subclassing links prototypes, it doesn't copy anything

The chain-lookup contract (the mental model to state): `obj.x` → own? → proto.x? → proto.proto.x? → ... → null → undefined. Assignment (obj.x = v) creates an OWN property (it never walks the chain to write — that's why setting a property doesn't affect other instances).

The API map, verified shapes:

```js
// SELECT
const el = document.querySelector('#app .item');      // first match
const all = document.querySelectorAll('.item');        // static NodeList
const byId = document.getElementById('app');          // fastest, id only
all.forEach(...)  // NodeList supports forEach (no spread needed)

// CREATE + INSERT
const li = document.createElement('li');
li.textContent = 'New item';                 // textContent: no HTML parsed — safe
li.className = 'item active';
list.append(li);                              // also: prepend, before, after

// REMOVE + REPLACE
li.remove();                                  // no parent traversal needed
el.replaceChildren(...newEls);                // idiom: clear + set in one op
el.innerHTML = '';                            // legacy clear

// THE EFFICIENCY PATTERNS
// 1. Build OFF-DOM, insert ONCE (each insert can reflow):
const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
items.forEach(i => { const li = ...; frag.append(li); });
list.append(frag);            // ONE DOM mutation, not N

// 2. Delegation for dynamic children (covered in its own question)
// 3. classList over className string surgery:
el.classList.add('on', 'visible'); el.classList.toggle('dark');

// 4. Security: textContent over innerHTML for ANY user-derived content
// innerHTML = '<li>' + userInput + '</li>'  ← XSS: userInput can close the tag
```

The reflow discipline (why the patterns exist): every mutation may invalidate layout; the browser batches and defers — UNLESS you force it by reading geometry (offsetHeight, getBoundingClientRect) after a pending write. Inserting a built fragment once keeps invalidation to a single reflow; inserting in a loop with reads between iterations is layout thrashing (verified mechanism in the render-pipeline question).

The mechanics, verified:

```js
// SYNC: throw unwinds until caught
try {
  null.x;                          // TypeError: cannot read properties of null
} catch (e) {
  e.name;      // 'TypeError'     — branch on error TYPE
  e.message;   // human detail
  e.stack;     // stack trace string (best-effort, not a standard)
} finally {
  // ALWAYS runs — verified: even a return in try completes finally first
}

// THE TYPE HIERARCHY (branch on it):
// null.x                    → TypeError       ✓ (verified)
// undeclaredVariable        → ReferenceError  ✓ (verified)
// JSON.parse('{bad')        → SyntaxError     ✓ (verified)

// ASYNC — the boundary that catches juniors:
try {
  fetch('/api').then(d => d.missing);   // rejection happens LATER
} catch (e) {
  // NEVER REACHED — the sync try/catch already exited
  // the error occurs in a future microtask, after this block finished
}
// CORRECT: catch in the async chain
fetch('/api').catch(e => ...);
// or with await:
try { const d = await fetch('/api'); }   // rejection → THIS catch ✓
catch (e) { ... }
```

The two events for what escaped (the production layer):
- window.onerror / 'error' event — sync errors that nothing caught
- window 'unhandledrejection' — Promise rejections with no .catch
These are where error-reporting SDKs (Sentry et al.) hook in — an uncaught async error does NOT crash the page (the event loop continues), it just never reaches your handler unless you subscribe to these events.

Design guidance (the judgment layer): catch what you can HANDLE — at the boundary where recovery is possible (retry, fallback UI, user message). Not everywhere (blanket try/catch swallows bugs), not nowhere (users see blank screens). Custom errors: class HttpError extends Error { constructor(status) { super(...); this.status = status; } } — the pattern that lets catch blocks branch on domain categories.

The phases, verified:

```
CLICK on <button> inside <div> inside <body>:

1. CAPTURE (top → down):  window → document → body → div   (capture listeners fire)
2. TARGET:               button's own listeners fire
3. BUBBLE (bottom → up): div → body → document → window   (default listeners)

addEventListener('click', fn)              // BUBBLE leg (default)
addEventListener('click', fn, true)       // CAPTURE leg
addEventListener('click', fn, {capture: true, once: true, passive: true})
```

The two stops + the one cancel (the distinction interviewers test):
```js
event.stopPropagation();
// NO further NODES see it — div's handler already ran? Then body's won't.
// Does NOT stop the default action — the link still navigates.

event.stopImmediatePropagation();
// Also cancels REMAINING handlers on the SAME element

button.addEventListener('click', e => e.preventDefault());
// The browser's built-in action is cancelled: the <a> doesn't navigate,
// the form doesn't submit, the checkbox still toggles? No — checkbox
// default (checking) IS cancelled by preventDefault.
// Independent axis: stopPropagation = propagation; preventDefault = default.
```

The listener options (the modern details):
- once: true — auto-remove after first fire (one-time intro popups)
- passive: true — promises the handler won't preventDefault → the browser doesn't wait for it before scrolling (the scroll-performance flag every touch/wheel handler should carry)
- capture — intercept on the way DOWN: useful to run BEFORE a child's handlers can stopPropagation (analytics that must see every click, security wrappers)

The non-bubbling exceptions (the gotcha list): focus/blur don't bubble (use focusin/focusout), load doesn't bubble, mouseenter/leave don't (use mouseover/out). The delegation question (covered separately) inherits this constraint — delegate only bubbling events.

The event loop, verified:

```js
setTimeout(() => console.log('setTimeout (macrotask)'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('Promise (microtask)'));
console.log('sync');
// Output — verified:
// sync            ← call stack first
// Promise (microtask)  ← microtasks before macrotasks
// setTimeout (macrotask) ← macrotask last
```

The model: JavaScript is single-threaded (one call stack — one thing at a time). Async operations (setTimeout, fetch, I/O) are handled by the HOST ENVIRONMENT (browser/Node), which queues their callbacks. The event loop is the dispatcher:

1. Run the call stack to completion (synchronous code first)
2. When the stack is empty: drain ALL microtasks (Promise callbacks, queueMicrotask)
3. Run ONE macrotask (setTimeout, I/O callback)
4. Drain microtasks again → repeat

The two queues:
- MICROTASK: Promise.then callbacks, queueMicrotask, MutationObserver — ALL run before the next macrotask
- MACROTASK: setTimeout, setInterval, I/O — one per tick

The consequence interviewers test: a long synchronous operation BLOCKS everything — no Promise callback, no timeout, nothing runs until the stack clears. 'JavaScript never preempts.'

The practical rules:
- Promise callbacks always fire before setTimeout callbacks (microtask > macrotask)
- setTimeout(fn, 0) is NOT immediate — it queues a macrotask; the current code + all microtasks run first
- The 'starvation' trap: infinite microtasks block macrotasks forever (the reason the spec separates them)

The three declarations, verified:

```js
if (true) {
  var v = 1;   // function-scoped — LEAKS outside this block
  let l = 2;   // block-scoped — stays here
  const c = 3; // block-scoped + must initialize
}
console.log(v);     // 1 (leaked — var ignores blocks)
// console.log(l); // ReferenceError — l is block-scoped

// Hoisting: var is usable (as undefined) before declaration
console.log(hoisted); // undefined (var hoisted, initialized to undefined)
var hoisted = 42;
// let/const hoist but stay in the Temporal Dead Zone until declaration
// console.log(tdz); // ReferenceError (TDZ)
let tdz = 'later';
```

The scoping difference (the interview core): var is function-scoped — the if-block's var v is visible OUTSIDE the block. let and const are block-scoped — they exist only within their nearest { }. This makes let/const predictable in loops and conditions; var was the classic source of unexpected-leak bugs.

The const subtlety (the most common follow-up): const means the BINDING is immutable, not the value. const arr = [1]; arr.push(2) is legal (the array mutates); arr = [3] is not (can't reassign). For true immutability: Object.freeze() or immutable patterns.

The decision rule: const by default (intent: won't reassign), let when reassignment is needed, var never (legacy code only).

The three concepts, verified:

```js
// HOISTING: var is usable as undefined before its line
console.log(hoisted); // undefined (var was hoisted + initialized to undefined)
var hoisted = 42;

// TDZ: let/const are known but NOT usable before their line
try {
  console.log(tdz);   // ReferenceError: Cannot access 'tdz' before initialization
} catch(e) { console.log(e.message) }
let tdz = 'now fine';

// SCOPE: var is function-scoped; let/const are block-scoped
function demo() {
  if (true) {
    var fnScoped = 1;   // visible in ALL of demo()
    let blockScoped = 2; // visible only in this block
  }
  console.log(fnScoped);    // 1 (leaked from the block)
  // console.log(blockScoped); // ReferenceError — block is gone
}
demo();
```

Hoisting, precisely: the engine processes declarations during compilation, BEFORE running the code. var declarations are hoisted and INITIALIZED to undefined (the variable exists as undefined from the function's start). let and const are hoisted but NOT initialized — they're in the TDZ from the block's start to the declaration line — accessing them throws ReferenceError.

The practical rules:
1. Using a var before its line: undefined (silent bug — usually a typo in a different order)
2. Using a let/const before its line: ReferenceError (the loud error — the design improvement)
3. Function declarations are fully hoisted (the body is available before the declaration line)
4. Function expressions are NOT hoisted (the variable follows its var/let rules)

The TDZ's purpose: to prevent the exact class of bug var enabled — accessing a variable before its declaration. By making it an error instead of undefined, the bug surfaces at development time, not as a NaN propagating through three functions.

The boundary, stated precisely:

```jsx
// SERVER COMPONENT — the default (no directive needed)
async function ProductPage({ id }) {
  const product = await db.products.get(id);   // direct data access ✓
  // useState here → ERROR. window here → ERROR. onClick here → ERROR.
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{product.name}</h1>                     {/* runs NEVER in the browser */}
      <AddToCartButton id={id} />                  {/* hand-off to client */}
    </div>
  );
}

// CLIENT COMPONENT — the opt-in directive
'use client';
function AddToCartButton({ id }) {
  const [adding, setAdding] = useState(false);   // interactivity lives HERE
  return <button onClick={() => setAdding(true)}>Add to cart</button>;
}
// AddToCartButton's code ships to the browser; ProductPage's doesn't.
```

The capability split (the interview core):

```
                     Server          Client
data fetching        direct await     via props/effects
secrets (db, keys)   ✓ safe           ✗ shipped
useState/effect      ✗               ✓
event handlers       ✗               ✓
window/localStorage  ✗               ✓
bundle cost          ZERO             JS to browser
serialize props      must be         n/a
```

The composition rules (where bugs come from):
- Server → Client: fine — pass serializable props (no functions, no class instances; plain data + Dates/Maps OK)
- Client → Server: NOT via import — a client component can't import a server one. Server code runs from client via ROUTE HANDLERS (route.ts API) or Server Actions
- 'use client' marks a BOUNDARY: everything imported below it is also client — one directive at a widget's root makes the whole widget client, which is why you place it at the leaves, not the page root

The migration instinct (the judgment layer): a page that's mostly content with small interactive islands should be ~90% server components. The anti-pattern: 'use client' on every page (App Router as Pages Router) — you pay the bundle + lose the streaming/direct-data wins for nothing.

The two models, verified:

```js
// CONTROLLED — React state is the truth, DOM follows
const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
<input value={email} onChange={e => setEmail(e.target.value)} />
// every keystroke: onChange → setState → re-render → input displays state
// the input CAN'T show anything state doesn't have (can't desync)

// UNCONTROLLED — DOM holds the truth, React reads on demand
const inputRef = useRef();
<input defaultValue="" ref={inputRef} />
const value = inputRef.current.value;  // read when needed (submit)
// typing doesn't re-render; the DOM just holds its own value
```

The trade-off table (the interview core):
- Source of truth: React state vs the DOM
- Re-renders: every keystroke vs none until read
- Instant validation per-keystroke: trivial (state always current) vs manual (listen to input events)
- Conditional formatting (input masks, capitalization): natural in controlled vs fights the DOM
- File inputs: CANNOT be controlled (browser security) — always uncontrolled
- Simple forms, huge forms: uncontrolled avoids re-render churn; libraries like react-hook-form use it
- Wiring cost: controlled needs value+onChange per input; uncontrolled needs refs + submit handler

The hybrid (the real-world answer): use controlled for inputs needing live reaction (validation, dependent fields, search-as-you-type) and uncontrolled for large forms where re-render cost matters — or react-hook-form, which keeps inputs uncontrolled (registering refs) and only pulls state at validation/submit, combining controlled ergonomics with uncontrolled performance.

The compilation, verified:

```js
// What you write:
const el = <div className="card">Hello {name}</div>;

// What the compiler emits (the truth of JSX):
const el = React.createElement(
  'div',                    // type: tag or component
  { className: 'card' },    // props
  'Hello ', name            // children (interpolation becomes args)
);

// What createElement returns — a plain object:
// { type: 'div', props: { className: 'card', children: ['Hello ', name] }, key: null }
```

The rules that follow from it being function calls:
- One root element per return (multiple siblings need a fragment `<>...</>` — one call needs one result)
- `className` not `class`, `htmlFor` not `for` (these are JS object props, and `class`/`for` are reserved or collide)
- Curly braces `{}` are JS expressions — anything valid in an expression works: `{items.map(i => <li key={i.id}>{i.name}</li>}`
- Components must start with a capital — `<div/>` is a string tag, `<Div/>` is the variable `Div`

The compile step (where it runs): a transpiler (Babel, or the compiler in your build tool) rewrites JSX before the browser sees it. Modern React (17+) imports `createElement` via the automatic JSX runtime — no React import needed just for JSX.

The split, verified:

```js
function Child({ label, onSave }) {   // props: label, onSave — from parent
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);  // state: owned here
  return (
    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
      {label}: {count}   {/* props = config, state = changing data */}
    </button>
  );
}
// <Child label="Clicks" onSave={fn} /> — parent controls label,
// but count belongs to Child. Child CANNOT do label = 'new'.
