The four contrasts that answer the question completely:
- Ownership: props belong to the parent; state belongs to the component
- Mutability: props are read-only inside the receiver; state changes via its setter
- Update path: props change when the PARENT re-renders with different values; state changes when the OWNER calls its setter
- Initial value: props come from the call site; state comes from useState's argument (first render only)

The derived-data rule (the common mistake to name): if a value can be computed from props or existing state, it should NOT be its own state — store it once, derive the rest. `const fullName = firstName + ' ' + lastName`, not a synced `fullName` state. Synced state is where bugs breed (two sources of truth drift).

The problem keys solve, verified:

```js
// WITHOUT keys (positional matching):
// [A, B, C] → [Z, A, B, C]   (Z inserted at front)
// React matches by POSITION: position-0 was A, now Z.
// Every node is torn down and rebuilt → all state lost, all inputs reset.

// WITH key={item.id} (identity matching):
// React sees A, B, C still exist (same keys) → their nodes are PRESERVED,
// only Z is created and inserted at the front. 3 nodes reused (verified).

{todos.map(todo => (
  <TodoRow key={todo.id} todo={todo} />   // stable identity from data
))}
```

The rules for good keys:
- Unique among siblings (not globally — two lists can both have key='a')
- Stable across renders (the same item keeps the same key)
- From the data (database id, slug) — NOT the array index

Why index keys are the trap (the interview's real question): index is stable only while the list never reorders. Insert at front → every item's index shifts → keys stop matching → React rebuilds everything. Worse: with stateful rows (an input inside each row), index matching makes row-3's state appear in what is now row-3 but is different data — user types in one row, deletes the row above, and their text 'moves' to a different item. Index keys are safe only when the list is static, never reordered, and rows have no state.

The mechanics and the traps, verified:

```js
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

// TRAP 1: rapid calls read stale state (batching)
// setCount(count+1); setCount(count+1); setCount(count+1);
// → count was 0 for all three → result 1, NOT 3 (verified)

// FIX: functional updates — read current at apply time
setCount(prev => prev + 1);  // 3 rapid clicks → 3 ✓

// TRAP 2: object mutation doesn't trigger re-render
// const [user, setUser] = useState({name:'A'});
// user.name = 'B'; setUser(user); — same reference → React sees no change
setUser({ ...user, name: 'B' });  // new object → re-render ✓

// TRAP 3: initializer runs on every render
const [x, setX] = useState(expensiveCompute());      // runs EVERY render
const [y, setY] = useState(() => expensiveCompute()); // lazy: runs once ✓
```

The batching model (why setters look async): React batches multiple setState calls in the same event handler into one re-render (18+: automatic batching everywhere, including Promises and timeouts). The state variable in your closure captures the value at render time — three queued updates all saw the old value. Functional updates `set(prev => next)` are the fix because the function receives the latest state when the batch applies.

The state-replacement model (the mental shift from class components): the setter REPLACES the value; it doesn't merge (unlike this.setState which shallow-mergeded). For objects/arrays you build the new value: `{...old, changed}` for objects, `[...old, item]` for arrays.

The model, verified:

```js
// A component is a function: props in, UI description out
function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <h1>Hello, {name}</h1>;  // WHAT to render, not HOW
}

// Same input → same output (the declarative contract)
// <Greeting name="Ada" /> always renders <h1>Hello, Ada</h1>
```

The three ideas that make React React:
- Components: UI split into reusable functions — a page is a tree of components
- Declarative: you write `UI = f(state)` — when state changes, React re-renders and diffs
- Virtual DOM: React keeps a lightweight copy of the UI tree, diffs old-vs-new, and applies only the changed DOM operations

The declarative contrast (what interviewers want you to articulate): in vanilla JS you imperatively find elements and mutate them (`document.getElementById('x').textContent = 'new'`); in React you never touch the DOM — you change state and describe the result. React reconciles the two trees and updates the real DOM minimally.

Why teams adopt it (the practical answer): component reuse across pages/products, a huge ecosystem (React Native for mobile, Next.js for SSR), one mental model for everything from widgets to apps, and hiring pool familiarity.

The anatomy, verified:

```js
// CUSTOM HOOK = function starting with 'use' + calls hooks inside
function useDebouncedValue(value, delay = 300) {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const t = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delay);
    return () => clearTimeout(t);   // CLEANUP: the previous timer dies on each change
  }, [value, delay]);
  return debounced;                  // stable return: just the value
}

// USE: each caller gets its OWN state — no sharing
function Search() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const debounced = useDebouncedValue(query, 300);
  useEffect(() => { if (debounced) search(debounced); }, [debounced]);
  // typing 'react' fires ONE search 300ms after the last keystroke (verified)
}
```

The rules it inherits (because it calls hooks):
- Only call at the top level of components or other hooks — never conditional, never in loops
- Name starts with `use` — this is also how the lint plugin enforces the rules for it

What a custom hook is NOT (the misconceptions to correct):
- NOT state sharing — two components calling useToggle() have independent toggles (verified: separate slot per component). Shared state needs lifting, context, or a store.
- NOT a component — no JSX, no render; it's logic extraction
- NOT free structure — the return shape is your API: a tuple [value, setter] for pair-shaped like useState, an object for multiple named results ({ data, loading, error } for data hooks)

The quality markers (what 'good' means):
1. Handles the full lifecycle: loading + error + cleanup (the unmount race: a fetch resolving after unmount must not setState — guard with an ignore flag or AbortController)
2. Stable references: memoize returned functions (useCallback) so callers' deps don't churn
3. Configurable via arguments, opinionated via defaults

The slot model (the why), verified:

```js
// React tracks hooks by POSITION in the call sequence:
// Render 1: [state:a] [state:b] [effect:c]   ← slots fill in order
// Render 2: [state:a] [state:b] [effect:c]   ← same order = same slots

// THE VIOLATION — a conditional hook:
function Bad({ showCount }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');        // slot 1 — always
  if (showCount) {
    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);    // slot 2 — SOMETIMES
  }
  useEffect(() => {});                        // slot 2 or 3 depending on the if!
}
// Render with showCount=true:  [name][count][effect]
// Render with showCount=false: [name][effect]
// The effect's slot now reads COUNT's state — hooks misalign.
// React detects it: 'Rendered fewer hooks than expected' → CRASH.
```

The two rules stated precisely:
1. Top level only — no if, no loops, no callbacks, no early returns before hooks
2. React functions only — components and custom hooks (functions named use*)

The correct pattern for the conditional need (what people are usually TRYING to do):
```js
function Good({ showCount }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);   // ALWAYS called — slot stable
  if (!showCount) return null;           // conditional RENDER, not hook
  return <div>{name} {count}</div>;       // early return AFTER hooks is fine
}
```
The loop need → fix by giving each iteration its own component (its own slot list) or composing hooks into a custom hook with a stable count.

The shape, verified:

```jsx
// CREATE the channel
const ThemeContext = createContext('light');   // default for no-provider reads

// PROVIDE at the subtree root
function App() {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('dark');
  // THE TRAP: this value literal creates a NEW object every render
  // → every consumer re-renders on EVERY App render, theme change or not
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ theme, setTheme }), [theme]);
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value={value}>
      <Page />          {/* no props drilled through here */}
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
