The flow (the interview structure):
```
child onChange → callback prop → parent setState → parent re-renders
  → new props flow down → children render the new value
```
Data down, events up — the unidirectional contract. The child never mutates; it ASKS. The parent decides — one place with update logic.

When you've lifted TOO far (the judgment half): if intermediate components only forward props (prop drilling), the fixes in preference order: (1) composition — pass children as props and skip the pass-through layers entirely, (2) lift only as high as the actual consumers need — the closest common ancestor, not the app root, (3) context for genuinely global low-frequency data, (4) a store with selectors when the tree is deep AND updates frequent.

The classic cases worth naming: controlled inputs in a form (each input's state lifted to the form so validation/submit see everything); sibling communication (a filter panel and a list both need the filter — lift to their shared container); and cache-like UI state (selected item shared by a master and detail pane).

