const mockSave = (user) => { calls.push(user); return Promise.resolve({id: 1}); };
async function register(user, save = realSave) { return save(user); }  // DI seam
await register({name: 'Al'}, mockSave);
expect(calls).toHaveLength(1);               // verified: boundary was called once
expect(calls[0].name).toBe('Al');            // with the right payload

// ASYNC — return or await; the runner handles rejection
// BAD:  test('x', () => { fetchThing().then(d => expect(d).toBe(1)); });  // exits BEFORE the assert
// GOOD: test('x', async () => { expect(await fetchThing()).toBe(1); });   // verified: waits

// SETUP/TEARDOWN — fresh state per test
let db;
beforeEach(() => { db = createDb(); });      // each test gets its own world
afterEach(() => { db.close(); });
```

The matcher families (the vocabulary):
- toBe (Object.is — identity/primitives) vs toEqual (deep structural) vs toStrictEqual (deep + type-strict)
- toBeTruthy/Falsy/Null/Undefined — for sentinel checks
- toHaveBeenCalled / toHaveBeenCalledWith / toHaveBeenCalledTimes — mock assertions
- expect(value).resolves / .rejects — promise outcomes

Vitest vs Jest (the modern choice): same API (test/expect/describe/mocks — near-drop-in), but Vitest runs on Vite's transform pipeline: no-Jest-config needed in a Vite app, ESM-native (no Babel interop), faster watch via HMR, and browser-mode testing. Jest: the default in React/TS templates, the bigger ecosystem of reporters/plugins. Same skills transfer — the patterns above are identical in both.

The pattern, verified shape:

```jsx
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';

// THE STRUCTURE: Arrange → Act → Assert
test('submitting the form calls save with the entered data', async () => {
  const save = jest.fn();                       // mock boundary
  render(<SignupForm onSave={save} />);          // Arrange: mount

  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/email/i), 'bo@x.com');  // Act: as a USER
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /submit/i }));

  expect(save).toHaveBeenCalledWith(             // Assert on BEHAVIOR
    expect.objectContaining({ email: 'bo@x.com' })
  );
});
```

What to test (the priority order):
1. User-visible behavior: interactions produce expected output — the form submits, the toggle switches, the error appears
2. State transitions the user SEES: loading → data → error states of a fetch hook/component
3. Edge cases in behavior: empty list renders 'no items', validation rejects bad input

What NOT to test (the discipline that separates seniors):
- NOT internal state (useState values) — test what the state CAUSES, not the state itself
- NOT implementation details (did the component call setX vs a reducer, class vs function)
- NOT snapshot-of-markup — breaks on every refactor, catches no real bugs

The query philosophy (RTL's core idea): query the way a USER finds things — by role (button, textbox), by label (form fields), by text. getByRole('button', {name: 'Submit'}) works regardless of markup changes; a CSS-selector query breaks when you rename a class. Accessibility-faithful queries also catch a11y bugs for free (a button no screen reader can find, a query can't find either).

The async mechanics (the practical details): everything after an interaction that triggers async (fetch resolving, a transition) needs `await` + `findBy*` queries (findByText = getByText + waitFor — retries until timeout). userEvent (user.type, user.click) over fireEvent: it fires the full real event sequence (focus → keydown → keypress → input), catching what fireEvent's single synthetic event misses.

The mechanics, verified conceptually:

```ts
// Without generics — the type information dies
function firstAny(arr: any[]): any { return arr[0]; }   // returns any: no checking

// With generics — the relationship is CAPTURED
function first<T>(arr: T[]): T { return arr[0]; }
const n = first([7, 8, 9]);    // n: number — narrowed, checked
const s = first(['a', 'b']);   // s: string
// verified at runtime: first([7,8,9]) → 7, first(['a','b']) → 'a'

// API wrapper — the everyday production use
async function fetchJson<T>(url: string): Promise<T> {
  const res = await fetch(url);
  return res.json() as Promise<T>;   // caller declares the expected shape
}
const user = await fetchJson<User>('/api/user');   // user: User
```

The generic constraint (bounding the parameter):
```ts
function logLength<T extends { length: number }>(x: T): T {
  console.log(x.length);   // guaranteed to exist ✓
  return x;
}
logLength('hi');     // T = string (has length)
logLength([1, 2]);   // T = number[]
// logLength(42);   // ERROR: number has no length
```

When NOT to use generics (the judgment interviewers probe): if the type parameter is used only ONCE and doesn't create a relationship, it adds ceremony without safety — a plain concrete type is clearer. Generics earn their cost when the SAME declaration serves many types while keeping each use's specific type intact (containers, wrappers, mappers over typed data).

The mechanics, verified:

```ts
