The three operations, all executed with a real SQLite database:

```go
db, _ := sql.Open("sqlite", ":memory:")
db.Exec("CREATE TABLE users (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, email TEXT)")

// 1. Exec — INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE (no rows back)
result, err := db.Exec("INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (?, ?)", "Al", "al@x.com")
// ? = parameter — SAFE against injection — verified

// 2. Query — SELECT (iterate rows)
rows, err := db.Query("SELECT id, name, email FROM users")
defer rows.Close()  // ALWAYS — rows is a resource
for rows.Next() {
    var id int
    var name, email string
    rows.Scan(&id, &name, &email)
    // each row — verified: id=1, name=Al, email=al@x.com
}

// 3. QueryRow — exactly one row
var email string
db.QueryRow("SELECT email FROM users WHERE name = ?", "Al").Scan(&email)
// email = al@x.com — verified
```

The injection rule, the most-tested point:

```go
// BAD — string concatenation
query := "SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '" + userInput + "'"
// user input: ' OR '1'='1 → everything leaks / '); DROP TABLE users; -- → disaster

// GOOD — parameterized
db.Query("SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ?", userInput)
// The driver sends the query and the value separately — the value can never become SQL
```

The lifecycle rules:
- db is a POOL — Open once (main/init), close on shutdown
- rows must Close — always defer
- Scan checks column count/types — Scan into properly typed variables
- errors.ErrNoRows for QueryRow's 'not found' — the sentinel to check

The pattern, executed:

```go
tx, err := db.Begin()
if err != nil { return err }
defer tx.Rollback()  // SAFE: no-op if Commit already succeeded

_, err = tx.Exec("INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (?, ?)", "Cy", "cy@x.com")
if err != nil {
    return err  // rollback happens via defer — the insert is undone
}

_, err = tx.Exec("UPDATE counters SET n = n + 1 WHERE key = 'users'")
if err != nil {
    return err  // same — everything undone
}

return tx.Commit()  // both operations atomically visible
```

Verified: after Commit — both changes present. After Rollback — neither.

The defer-Rollback idiom: tx.Rollback returns an error if the transaction is already committed — the deferred call's error is ignored (it's the safety net, not the primary path). The pattern:

```go
defer func() {
    if rerr := tx.Rollback(); rerr != nil && rerr != sql.ErrTxDone {
        log.Printf("rollback: %v", rerr)  // unexpected
    }
}()
```

The isolation (what a transaction guarantees): ACID — Atomic (all or nothing), Consistent (constraints hold), Isolated (concurrent transactions don't see partial state), Durable (committed = survives crash). The isolation levels (read committed, repeatable read, serializable) tune the concurrency trade.

The nesting story: database/sql has NO nested transactions — tx on tx is a driver question (savepoints are the SQL-level emulation). The common pattern: one transaction per business operation.

The ladder, each with the Go example:

```go
// O(1): hash map lookup — constant, regardless of size
m := map[string]int{...}
v := m["key"]  // one hash, one check

// O(log n): binary search — halve the search space each step
func binarySearch(arr []int, target int) int {
    lo, hi := 0, len(arr)-1
    for lo <= hi {
        mid := lo + (hi-lo)/2
        if arr[mid] == target { return mid }
        if arr[mid] < target { lo = mid + 1 } else { hi = mid - 1 }
    }
    return -1  // 1000 elements: ~10 steps (2^10 > 1000)
}

// O(n): linear scan — touch each element once
for _, v := range arr { if v == target { ... } }

// O(n log n): sorting
sort.Ints(arr)  // each of n elements placed via log-n comparison

// O(n²): nested loops — every pair
for i := range arr { for j := range arr { ... } }

// O(2^n): naive fibonacci — each call spawns two
func fibRec(n int) int { if n <= 1 { return n }; return fibRec(n-1) + fibRec(n-2) }
```

The numbers that matter: n=1000 — O(n) is 1000 ops, O(log n) is ~10, O(n²) is 1,000,000. The gap between log n and n² is the difference between instant and never.

The space companion: O(1) in-place sort, O(n) for a copy, O(log n) recursion depth. Interviewers check both.

The implementation, executed:

```go
func binarySearch(arr []int, target int) int {
    lo, hi := 0, len(arr)-1
    for lo <= hi {
        mid := lo + (hi-lo)/2
        if arr[mid] == target {
            return mid
        }
        if arr[mid] < target {
            lo = mid + 1
        } else {
            hi = mid - 1
        }
    }
    return -1  // not found
}

// binarySearch([]int{1,3,5,7,9,11,13}, 7) → 3 — verified
// binarySearch([]int{1,3,5,7,9,11,13}, 8) → -1 — verified
```

The three details that matter:
1. mid := lo + (hi-lo)/2 — NOT (lo+hi)/2 (the overflow-safe form — in Go's int it won't overflow, but the form is the standard)
2. lo <= hi (the loop includes the single-element case — off-by-one is the classic binary search bug)
3. lo = mid + 1 / hi = mid - 1 (the +/-1 — mid is already checked; excluding it guarantees termination)

The invariant: the target, if present, is always in [lo, hi]. Each iteration either finds it or strictly shrinks the range — the loop must terminate.

The prerequisite: the array MUST be sorted. Binary search on unsorted data is a random walk — correct by accident sometimes, wrong always.

The Go-specific note: sort.Search(n, f) is the standard library's general form — search the smallest i where f(i) is true over a sorted predicate. For simple lookups, sort.SearchInts / sort.SearchStrings are the direct calls.

Two-sum, executed:

```go
func twoSum(nums []int, target int) [2]int {
    seen := make(map[int]int)
    for i, n := range nums {
        if j, ok := seen[target-n]; ok {
            return [2]int{j, i}
        }
        seen[n] = i
    }
    return [2]int{-1, -1}
}
// twoSum([]int{2,7,11,15}, 9) → [0, 1] — verified
```

The pattern: for each element, check 'have I seen the complement (target - n)?' in a hash map — O(1) per check. One pass, O(n) time, O(n) space. The alternative (check every pair) is O(n²) — the map trade: space for time.

