slices.Sort(users)  // by a natural ordering (Ordered constraint)
slices.SortFunc(users, func(a, b User) int { return a.Age - b.Age })  // by comparison
```

The complexity: O(n log n), in-place (the slice's memory is rearranged, not copied).

The reverse: sort.Slice with the flipped comparison, or sort.Reverse(...) for the interface form.

The converging pattern — palindrome check, executed:

```go
func isPalindrome(s string) bool {
    for i, j := 0, len(s)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
        if s[i] != s[j] { return false }
    }
    return true
}
// isPalindrome("racecar") → true — verified
// isPalindrome("hello") → false — verified
```

The pattern: two pointers starting at both ends, moving toward each other. Each iteration compares, then advances. The loop ends when they cross. O(n) time, O(1) space.

When two-pointer wins: the data is ORDERED (sorted, or symmetric like a palindrome) and the answer is a pair or a position — the ordering lets you decide which pointer to move from the comparison, skipping n-1 pairs per step.

The three variants:
1. Converging (i from start, j from end) — palindrome, sorted two-sum (if the array is sorted: too small → i++, too big → j--)
2. Fast/slow (same direction, different speeds) — cycle detection (they meet), middle-finding (fast ends, slow is mid)
3. Dual-array (one pointer per array) — sorted merge, intersection

The trade vs. the hash map: two-sum on an UNSORTED array with no ordering — hash map O(n) time, O(n) space. Two-sum on a SORTED array — two-pointer O(n) time, O(1) space. The sortedness buys the space saving.

The error interface is one method. A struct that adds it becomes an error, and its fields travel with the failure:

```go
type NotFoundError struct {
    Name string
}

func (e *NotFoundError) Error() string {
    return "not found: " + e.Name
}

// returning it
func lookup(name string) error {
    return &NotFoundError{Name: name}
}
```

The caller's side is errors.As — the type-aware chain walk:

```go
err := lookup("cfg")
var nfe *NotFoundError
if errors.As(err, &nfe) {
    fmt.Println("which one:", nfe.Name)   // the field the caller needed
}
```

That's the whole pattern: struct for the data, Error() for the message, pointer receiver for the method, errors.As at the consumer. The value over a plain errors.New string is exactly the fields — 'which record failed' stops being prose in a message and becomes data the caller branches on.

Go made a language-design bet: failures should be visible in the code, not hidden in a throw path. So a Go function that can fail says so in its return values, and the caller's first order of business is checking:

```go
data, err := os.ReadFile(path)
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("load config: %w", err)
}
// data is valid here — no ambiguity
```

That guard is the entire error-handling mechanism. There's no catch block, no finally, no exception hierarchy to know — just a value check where the failure occurred. The pattern repeats at every layer, and each layer chooses: return it up (wrapped with context), handle it (retry, default, log), or crash (genuinely unrecoverable).

What the pattern buys is auditability: a reader can see every failure path of a function by reading the function — no stack of handlers elsewhere, no documentation of what throws. The cost is the visible ceremony — Go code has more if err != nil lines than exception code has catch keywords, and that verbosity is the most-criticized and most-defended property of the language.

The deeper point for an interview: this isn't boilerplate to minimize; it's control flow made literal. Every line of error handling is a decision the author made at a specific failure point — which is exactly what code review wants to see.

Go's founders looked at exception systems and rejected them for Go. Instead, an error is an ordinary value — usually the last return — and every caller decides, right at the call, what to do about it. The function signature itself tells you it can fail; there's no invisible throw path to go hunting for.

```go
f, err := os.Open(path)
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("load config: %w", err)
}
```

That explicitness is the design. With exceptions, a call that can fail looks identical to one that can't — the failure exits through a side channel the compiler doesn't force you to acknowledge. With (value, error), the check is three lines you can see, and unused-variable rules mean you can't accidentally ignore the error entirely without the compiler noticing.

The cost is honest: Go code has more visible if err != nil branches than equivalent exception code has catch blocks. But the branches are where the handling actually lives, and their visibility is what keeps failure paths reviewed. You get to complain about the verbosity; you don't get surprise control flow.

Go splits failure into two kinds, and the choice depends on who can fix it. If the caller could do something about it — retry, show a message, fall back — return an error value. A file that doesn't exist, a request with bad input, a database that's down: those are expected failures, so (result, error) is the contract.

A panic says something is wrong with the program itself. An index that should always be valid because you proved the math, a map that was initialized in the same function, a switch that handled every case — if those fail, no caller can fix it; the code is wrong. That's what panic is for.

The practical line I use: panic when the bug would be silently corrupting if continued; error when the failure is a legitimate outcome of the request. A library that panics on user input pushes its bugs into every caller's crash log — which is why well-behaved packages convert even their internal panics to errors at the package boundary.

```go
// error: caller can handle — return it
if err := save(); err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("save: %w", err)
}
// panic: invariant broken — the program is wrong
index := computeIndex()  // PROVEN safe by construction
_ = items[index]         // if this panics, the proof was wrong
```

The scenario, written out:

```go
func f() {
    defer fmt.Println("1st registered")
    defer fmt.Println("2nd registered")
    defer func() {
        if r := recover(); r != nil {
            fmt.Println("recovered:", r)
        }
    }()
    // Output order:
    //   1. "2nd registered" — LIFO
    //   2. "1st registered"
    //   3. "recovered: <panic>"
    // (recovery happens in the LAST defer — the first to run)
    panic("boom")
}
```

Wait — the recovery defer is LAST registered, so it runs FIRST. The order: recovery defer (stops the panic), then "2nd", then "1st". The recovery catch happens before the other defers run — the unwind stops at the recovering defer, and the remaining defers in the stack still execute as normal returns.

The precise sequence on panic:
1. The panic unwinds: deferred calls pop LIFO
2. The LAST-registered defer (typically the recover wrapper) runs FIRST
3. recover() returns the panic value — unwinding stops
4. The deferred function returns — f continues returning normally
5. Any remaining defers (earlier-registered ones) still run — they're in the normal return path

The interview gotcha: if the recover defer was registered BEFORE the resource defers (reversed order), the resources close BEFORE the recover runs — the ordering is structural, not magical.

The scenario with the wrapping chain:

```go
func processOrder(orderID string) error {
    user, err := loadUser(orderID)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("process order %s: load user: %w", orderID, err)  // layer 1 context
    }

    charge, err := chargePayment(orderID, user)
    if err != nil {
        // payment failed — decide the ACTION, not just the return
        if isRetryable(err) {
            return fmt.Errorf("process order %s: charge: %w", orderID, err)  // upstream retries
        }
        return fmt.Errorf("process order %s: charge: %w", orderID, err)  // no retry for card declined
    }

    if err := publishQueue(orderID); err != nil {
        log.Printf("queue publish failed for %s (non-fatal): %v", orderID, err)
        // Queue failure: the ORDER is processed — don't fail the whole flow
        // (outbox pattern: record locally, retry later)
    }
    return nil
}
```

The decisions in this scenario:
1. EACH layer wraps with ITS context (the order ID, the operation) — %w everywhere
2. The caller sees the full chain: 'process order 123: charge: connection refused'
3. Retryable vs terminal — checked and acted on at the layer with the knowledge
4. Queue failure — the business outcome succeeded (payment charged); the notification is eventually-consistent (log + outbox), not a hard fail

The errors.As pattern at the top: if the API layer needs the specific error type:
var apiErr *APIError
if errors.As(err, &apiErr) { if apiErr.Code == 429 { /* backoff */ } }

The anti-pattern: wrapping everything as a generic 'something failed' — every layer adding context but nobody adding facts.

The scenario numbers:

```go
func handler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    // Each request IS a goroutine (net/http spawns one per connection)
    data, err := slowService(r.Context())  // this blocks the GOROUTINE, not the thread
    json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(data)
}

http.ListenAndServe(":8080", mux)
// 10,000 concurrent requests:
//   - 10,000 goroutines (2KB each = ~20MB — fine)
//   - GOMAXPROCS OS threads (16 cores = 16 threads actually RUNNING Go code)
//   - Blocked-on-I/O goroutines park (the thread handles others)
//   - The DB and the downstream service see the bottleneck, not the server
```

The scheduler's mechanics for this scenario: 10,000 G (goroutines) distributed across 16 P (scheduler contexts, one per core). Each P has a local run queue. A goroutine blocking on the DB call parks in the network poller — the M (thread) it was running on picks the next G from the P's queue. The 16 threads are always busy with the goroutines that are actually runnable.

The contrast with thread-per-request (Java's classic model / C's): 10,000 threads × 1MB stack = 10GB reserved; context-switching across 10,000 threads = the scheduler thrash; the OS caps (ulimit) hit far earlier. The goroutine model makes the concurrency level a non-issue — the SERVER's bottleneck moves to where it should be (the DB, the downstream).

The genuine limits to name in an interview: the downstream resources. 10,000 concurrent requests = 10,000 DB connections (the DB pool — max 25 connections with queuing), 10,000 open files (the fd limit), 10,000 DNS lookups. Go removes the language-level limit; the system's other limits remain.

The two shapes, side by side:

```go
// Value receiver: returns a new, the original untouched (immutable semantics)
func (c Counter) Incremented() Counter {
    c.n++          // increments the COPY — the method's own c
    return c       // returns the modified copy
}

// Pointer receiver: mutates in place (the caller's counter changes)
func (c *Counter) Increment() {
    c.n++          // through the pointer — the CALLER's c
}

// Both, used:
c1 := Counter{0}
c2 := c1.Incremented()  // c1: {0}, c2: {1} — pure
c1.Increment()          // c1: {1} — mutated
```

The choice by the receiver's semantics:

VALUE (immutable): small types (time.Time, image.Point — values the language treats as numbers); the copy is the FEATURE (the caller's data can't change through you); the method returns the result.

POINTER (mutate): in-place updates (the state change IS the operation — Increment on a counter); large structs (copy cost); consistency (a type with ANY pointer method should make all methods pointer — the method-set interface rule).

The consistency rule, which is the real answer: the receiver kind is the type's identity statement (value OR reference). Mixing kinds splits the method set (interface satisfaction differs) and confuses every caller. Pick the identity, apply uniformly.

The library calibration: time.Time (all value — immutable); bytes.Buffer (all pointer — mutable reference). Each is ONE thing.

The anatomy:

```go
func divide(a, b int) (int, error)
//  func    keyword
//  divide  name (not part of the type)
//  a, b    parameter names (docs only — not the type)
//  int     the shared parameter type
//  (int, error)  the results
```

The signature AS A TYPE — what matters for assigning functions to variables, passing them, comparing:

```go
type MathFunc func(int, int) int

add := func(a, b int) int { return a + b }
var m MathFunc = add   // fits the signature type
m(3, 4)                // 7 — executed
```

func(int, int) int IS a type — a function type. Variable names in signatures are documentation (godoc shows them, callers read them), but two functions with the same types and different names are the SAME type.

The signature variants: single result can drop the parens (func f() int); multiple results need them (func f() (int, error)); zero results (func f()); variadic (func f(nums ...int) — the last parameter);
named results (func f() (result int, err error) — zero-initialized, naked-returnable, defer-mutable).

The comparison boundary: function types are comparable only to nil (func values compare false against nil, anything else is a compile error). They're not equatable values — two identical functions aren't == comparable (you compare by calling or by identity through pointers if you must).

The form:

```go
func divide(a, b int) (int, error) {
    if b == 0 {
        return 0, fmt.Errorf("division by zero")
    }
    return a / b, nil
}

q, err := divide(10, 2)   // 5, nil
q, err = divide(10, 0)    // 0, error
```

Executed. The convention the whole ecosystem follows: error is the LAST return value; on failure, the other values are zero/nil (don't trust them); on success, error is nil. Check err before using the values.

questions.deep:
3. Defer modification — THE killer use (the defer topic's core): a deferred function can read and WRITE the named returns. The cleanup/inspect pattern:

```go
func get() (result int, err error) {
    defer func() {
        if err != nil { result = 0 }  // scrub on failure
    }()
    result, err = doWork()
    return
}
```

Without the NAME, the defer couldn't touch the return value — this pattern REQUIRES named returns.

4. Documentation: func Read(p []byte) (n int, err error) — the docs reference n and err by name — godoc shows them; callers read what each value IS.

questions.spoken:
The mechanics, executed:

```go
func main() {
    defer func() {
        if r := recover(); r != nil {
            fmt.Println("recovered:", r)   // "recovered: boom"
        }
    }()
    panic("boom")
    // never reached
}
// prints: recovered: boom
// — then main returns NORMALLY; the program continues
```

The pattern, executed:

```go
func readFile(name string) error {
    f, err := os.Open(name)
    if err != nil {
        return err       // error path — no leak (nothing acquired yet)
    }
    defer f.Close()     // EVERY exit from here: file closes

    // ... work with f ...
    return nil
}
```

The discipline: defer f.Close() comes AFTER the error check (you can't close a nil file) and BEFORE any other return path — that's the invariant: every Open followed immediately by its deferred Close.

The subtlety interviewers probe: Close returns an error too (buffered writes may fail on close — deferred close silently discards it). For read-only files, the discard is safe. For writes, the pattern that surfaces close errors:

```go
func writeFile(name string) error {
    f, err := os.Create(name)
    if err != nil { return err }
    defer f.Close()

    if _, err := f.WriteString("data"); err != nil {
        return err       // f.Close runs via defer
    }
    return f.Close()    // explicit close to catch the error
}
```

The pattern with a named return + defer is the general form:
```go
func writeFile(name string) (err error) {
    f, err := os.Create(name)
    if err != nil { return err }
    defer func() {
        if cerr := f.Close(); cerr != nil && err == nil {
            err = cerr  // surface the close error
        }
    }()
    // ... work ...
    return
}
```

The general resource rule: whatever has a Cleanup/Close/Unlock, the defer goes at acquisition — files, network connections, mutexes, response bodies (the HTTP client's resp.Body defer is the same discipline).

The round-trip, executed:

```go
type User struct {
    Name  string `json:"name"`
    Email string `json:"email"`
    Age   int    `json:"age,omitempty"`  // skip when 0
}

// Marshal: Go → JSON
u := User{Name: "Al", Email: "al@x.com"}
b, _ := json.Marshal(u)
// {"name":"Al","email":"al@x.com"} — Age omitted (omitempty, zero value) — verified

// Unmarshal: JSON → Go
var parsed User
json.Unmarshal(b, &parsed)
// parsed.Name == "Al", parsed.Email == "al@x.com" — verified
```

The struct tag is the name mapping: `json:"name"` says 'this Go field serializes as the JSON key \"name\".' Without a tag, the Go field name is used as-is. The options: `omitempty` (skip zero values), `-` (never serialize), `json:"name,string"` (encode as a JSON string even for numbers).

The streaming alternatives for large data: json.NewDecoder(reader).Decode(&v) (parse from a stream — HTTP bodies, files) and json.NewEncoder(writer).Encode(v) (write to a stream — HTTP responses). The batch forms (Marshal/Unmarshal) work on complete []byte — fine for everything that fits in memory.

The error cases every handler must handle: syntax error (bad JSON — `not json` → error, verified) and type mismatch (JSON string into Go int → error, verified). Both are the CLIENT's fault — 400 in an HTTP context, never 500.

It's random on purpose. The runtime picks a random starting bucket for each iteration, so the same map prints in different orders on different runs. Early Go had a deterministic order; people wrote code that depended on it; the Go team made it explicitly randomized so that dependency could never form silently. Now the spec says order is unspecified — treat every iteration order as an accident.

When order matters, the pattern is fixed and small:

```go
keys := make([]string, 0, len(m))
for k := range m {
    keys = append(keys, k)
}
sort.Strings(keys)
for _, k := range keys {
    fmt.Println(k, m[k])
}
```

The reason this matters in interviews and code review: code that 'worked' in testing with 3 keys can break in production with 300, because iteration order differences expose hidden assumptions — first-key-wins logic, dedup-by-iteration, pretty-printing diffs. If two runs of a test on the same map can disagree, the test is testing an accident.

The loop gives you two things per iteration: the position and a copy of the element at that position. The copy is the trap — anything you do to it stays done to the copy only.

```go
type Task struct{ Done bool }
tasks := []Task{{false}, {false}}

for _, t := range tasks {
    t.Done = true      // modifies the COPY
}
fmt.Println(tasks[0].Done)  // false — nothing changed
```

The fix is to use the index and write back through it:

```go
for i := range tasks {
    tasks[i].Done = true   // writes through to the slice's storage
}
```

or range over a slice of pointers, where the copy is the pointer — pointing at the same underlying struct, so field writes land.

The `_` in the buggy version is where the tell lives: by discarding the index you lose the only write-back handle. `for i := range s` with `s[i]` is the pattern that can't fail this way — same simplicity, no copy hazard.

questions.deep:
Why does range copy the element? Because the loop variable is an ordinary variable, and range's assignment into it is ordinary assignment — for value types (structs, ints, strings), assignment copies. The loop var is written fresh each iteration from the slot, and your body writes to that fresh copy, not the slot.

Watch the trap with a struct slice (executed):
```go
type Task struct{ Done bool }
tasks := []Task{{false}, {false}}
for _, t := range tasks {
    t.Done = true
}
fmt.Println(tasks[0].Done) // false — the loop never touched the slice
```
The write landed in t — a stack variable the runtime reuses per iteration — and the slice's backing array never saw it. Contrast the fix, `for i := range tasks { tasks[i].Done = true }`: here there's no element copy at all (you use the index), and the write goes through the slice's storage directly.

The pointer-slice case explains the boundary: `[]*Task` ranges with the copy being the POINTER — 8 bytes, pointing at the same Task. Field writes through the copy land on the shared struct. So 'range copies the element' is precisely true, and for pointers the copy is a reference — the aliasing you want.

Go 1.22 note: the loop variable is now fresh per iteration (the capture-bug fix), but it is STILL a copy of the element for value types — the two changes are orthogonal, and the copy-trap survives.

questions.spoken:
The range form is the idiomatic iteration, and its two returns carry the subtlety:

```go
s := "héllo"
for i, r := range s {
    fmt.Println(i, r)
    // 0 104 — 'h' at byte 0
    // 1 233 — 'é' at byte 1 (2 bytes wide)
    // 3 108 — 'l' at byte 3 — offset JUMPED past é
    // 4 108, 5 111
}
```

Executed. The index is a BYTE OFFSET — advancing by each rune's UTF-8 width. The value is the decoded code point — safe for character logic.

The byte-level alternative:

```go
for i := 0; i < len(s); i++ {
    b := s[i]      // 104, 195, 169, 108, 108, 111
}
```

No offset jumps — but values at multi-byte positions are encoding fragments, not characters.

The mistake being tested: combining the classic loop with character comparisons — s[i] == 'é' never fires (s[i] is byte 195, not rune 233). That class of bug is why range is the default.

Embedding is declaring a field with no explicit name — the type name becomes it:

```go
type User struct{ Name string; Age int }
func (u User) Greet() string { return "hi " + u.Name }

type Admin struct {
    User       // embedded — field name IS 'User'
    Level int
}

a := Admin{User: User{Name: "Root", Age: 40}, Level: 9}
fmt.Println(a.Name, a.Greet(), a.Level)  // Root hi Root 9 — promoted
```

Executed. Both the field (Name) and the method (Greet) arrive on Admin. That promotion is the feature: the embedded type's exported fields and methods become part of the outer type's surface, reachable without naming the embedded type — unless shadowed by an outer declaration.

What embedding provides, precisely: reuse (Admin carries User's implementation), promotion (unqualified access), interface satisfaction through promotion (if User satisfies Greeter, Admin does too — the embedded methods count in Admin's method set), and delegation wiring (embed an interface, and the struct satisfies it while forwarding to the concrete value stored in the field).

What it is NOT, and the distinction is the interview's core: inheritance. No subtype (Admin is not a User — no assignment, no polymorphism), no overriding (an outer Greet SHADOWS; User's remains via a.User.Greet()), no dynamic dispatch (method calls bind to the receiver type statically). Embedding is composition with sugar — the sugar being that the inner's surface appears on the outer.

Go interfaces are satisfied implicitly. The type declares its methods; the interface declares its method list; if the type's method set contains all of the interface's methods, the type satisfies it — and the compiler verifies this at the assignment where you use the type as the interface.

```go
type Reader interface{ Read() string }

type File struct{ name string }
func (f File) Read() string { return "reading " + f.name }

var r Reader = File{"a.txt"}
fmt.Println(r.Read())
```

Executed. No declaration connects File to Reader — not at File's definition, not at Reader's, nowhere. The fit is verified where it's used, and only there.

What this buys: interfaces can be defined AFTER the types that satisfy them. You can write an interface for types in a package you don't own — sort.Interface over your type, io.Reader over anything with a Read. The dependency arrow points the useful direction: consumers define the interface they need; producers just have methods.

The standard library is built on this: io.Reader (one method) is satisfied by files, network connections, buffers, cipher streams, HTTP bodies, decompressors — most written without any reference to io.Reader. The interface came later, and the fit was discovered, not declared.

