questions.spoken:
range over a channel is a receive loop the compiler writes for you:

```go
for v := range ch {
    process(v)
}
```

Each iteration is a receive; the loop ends when the channel delivers the end-signal — closed and drained. There is NO other exit: no condition clause, no break-on-error, nothing to configure. The loop either runs until close or blocks forever on an empty open channel.

That's why range over channels requires the close discipline: the producer MUST close (typically defer close at the top of the producing function), or every downstream range leaks into a permanent block. The pairing is the idiom:

```go
func produce(out chan<- int) {
    defer close(out)  // the range's termination signal
    for _, v := range data {
        out <- v
    }
}

// consumer
for v := range out {   // terminates exactly when produce returns
    use(v)
}
```

The mechanics under the loop: range is the comma-ok receive — each iteration does v, ok := <-ch; ok true → body; ok false → exit. Buffered values drain normally (ok true for each); the close is seen only when the buffer empties. So range gets the lossless-drain-then-terminate guarantee the close semantics provide.

Where range DOESN'T fit: when the consumer has multiple channels, needs timeouts, or wants to stop early — those are select's jobs, with the comma-ok form explicitly written. range is the single-channel, run-to-completion loop — the simplest correct consumer, provided the producer owns the close.

The pattern, executed:

```go
func withHeader(next http.HandlerFunc) http.HandlerFunc {
    return func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
        w.Header().Set("X-Custom", "yes")   // BEFORE — pre-processing
        next(w, r)                            // the actual handler
        // AFTER — post-processing (rare — response already sent)
    }
}

// wrap the handler
mux.HandleFunc("/mw", withHeader(myHandler))
// GET /mw → handler sees X-Custom: yes — verified
```

The shape: a function that RETURNS a function (the wrapper). The wrapper runs before the handler (pre: logging, auth, headers, request ID) and optionally after (post: response metrics — the response is already sent, so post is mostly cleanup).

The standard middleware chain — composable:

```go
func chain(h http.HandlerFunc, mw ...func(http.HandlerFunc) http.HandlerFunc) http.HandlerFunc {
    for i := len(mw) - 1; i >= 0; i-- {  // wrap in reverse
        h = mw[i](h)
    }
    return h
}
h := chain(handler, logging, auth, headers)
```

The common middlewares every service has: logging (method, path, status, duration), auth (check token, reject or attach to context), panic recovery (defer recover → 500), request ID (generate + set in context and response header).

The http.Handler interface variant: middleware can also be func(http.Handler) http.Handler — the more general form (works on muxes, not just handlers). The two forms compose; pick one per codebase.

A mutex guarantees exactly one goroutine in the critical section:

```go
var (
    counter int
    mu      sync.Mutex
)

func increment() {
    mu.Lock()
    counter++
    mu.Unlock()
}

// 100 goroutines → counter exactly 100 (verified with mutex)
// 100 goroutines without → ~90 (verified — lost updates)
```

Without the mutex: counter++ is three operations (read, add, write); goroutines interleave and updates vanish. With: exactly 100, every time.

The rules: defer mu.Unlock() right after Lock (runs on every exit path); var mu sync.Mutex is ready (zero-value, no constructor); don't copy a mutex (go vet's copylocks catches it).

The mistake to name: incrementing without the lock because it's just one line — one line is three operations, and the update vanishes silently.

The pattern, executed:

```go
func TestDouble(t *testing.T) {
    tests := []struct {
        name string
        in   int
        want int
    }{
        {"positive", 5, 10},
        {"zero", 0, 0},
        {"negative", -3, -6},
    }
    for _, tc := range tests {
        t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {
            if double(tc.in) != tc.want {
                t.Errorf("double(%d) = %d, want %d", tc.in, double(tc.in), tc.want)
            }
        })
    }
}
```

Executed output: each subtest named and passed:
```
=== RUN   TestDouble/positive
=== RUN   TestDouble/zero
=== RUN   TestDouble/negative
--- PASS: TestDouble (with subtests)
```

The advantages that made it THE Go pattern:
1. Add a case = add one struct literal — no copy-paste of test functions
2. t.Run creates SUBTESTS — each case reports separately; the failure tells you WHICH case (TestDouble/negative FAILED)
3. Run a single case: go test -run TestDouble/negative
4. The test data and the assertion logic are SEPARATE — the table is the specification

The conventions: a `name` field for readability; the anonymous struct in the function (or a named one for reuse); t.Run always (not a plain for loop — the subtest isolation is the point).

