The snippet below walks the same path the JVM does — a `put` that hashes to a bucket, an overwrite when the key `equals()` an existing one, and a resize once the table fills past its threshold.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class HashMapInternals {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();  // cap 16, lf 0.75
        map.put("apple", 1);   // hash("apple") -> index -> bucket
        map.put("apple", 2);   // same key (equals) -> overwrite to 2
        map.put("grape", 9);   // different bucket (or collision -> chain)

        // get repeats the hashing, then equals() pinpoints the key:
        int v = map.get("apple");  // 2

        // After ~12 entries (16 * 0.75) the table resizes to 32.
        for (int i = 0; i < 20; i++) map.put("k" + i, i);
    }
}
```
Nothing here is special syntax — it is plain Java 17. The machinery (hashing, chaining, resizing) all runs inside `put` and `get`, invisible to the caller.

The classic interview use is finding the first repeated value in one pass. Read the return value of `add()` and stop as soon as it is `false`.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class DuplicateDetection {
    // Find the first duplicate in O(n) time, O(n) space
    static Long findFirstDuplicate(List<Long> orderIds) {
        var seen = new HashSet<Long>();
        for (var id : orderIds) {
            if (!seen.add(id)) {   // add returned false -> duplicate
                return id;
            }
        }
        return null;               // no duplicates
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var orders = List.of(101L, 102L, 103L, 102L, 104L);
        System.out.println("First duplicate: " + findFirstDuplicate(orders)); // 102

        // Process each id exactly once -> act only when add returns true
        var processed = new HashSet<Long>();
        for (var id : orders) {
            if (processed.add(id)) {
                System.out.println("Processing: " + id);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The `!seen.add(id)` line is the trick: one call inserts the id and tells you whether it was already there, with no extra `contains()` lookup.

