JPA is a **specification** in `jakarta.persistence` — a portable API. Hibernate is one of its **implementations** that adds non-standard features (second-level cache, `@Formula`, criteria extensions). Code that uses only JPA types is portable; code that imports `org.hibernate.*` is locked to Hibernate.

```java
// ---------- 100% JPA (portable to EclipseLink, OpenJPA, DataNucleus) ----------
@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {
    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY) private Long id;
    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true) private String email;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Order> orders = new ArrayList<>();
}

@Repository
public class UserDao {
    @PersistenceContext private EntityManager em;      // JPA API
    public List<User> findByDomain(String domain) {
        return em.createQuery(
            "SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.email LIKE :d", User.class)   // JPQL (portable)
            .setParameter("d", "%@" + domain).getResultList();
    }
}

// ---------- Hibernate-only (not portable) ----------
@Entity
@org.hibernate.annotations.Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_WRITE)  // Hibernate
@org.hibernate.annotations.DynamicUpdate                                        // Hibernate
public class Product {
    @Id private Long id;

    @org.hibernate.annotations.Formula("(SELECT AVG(r.stars) FROM reviews r WHERE r.product_id = id)")
    private Double avgRating;   // computed column — Hibernate extension
}

// Dropping to the native Hibernate Session:
@Autowired private EntityManager em;
Session session = em.unwrap(Session.class);
session.enableFilter("active").setParameter("flag", true);   // Hibernate filters
```

- Every Spring Data JPA project transitively uses **Hibernate** as the provider unless explicitly switched. You are already on Hibernate — the question is whether your code *depends* on its extensions.
- Stay in `jakarta.persistence.*` for 95% of code; reach for `org.hibernate.*` only when you need features JPA does not define (multi-tenancy filters, `@Formula`, `@Any`).
- JPA's `EntityManager` is a thin wrapper — `em.unwrap(Session.class)` gives you the Hibernate `Session` for advanced use without rewriting repositories.

Spring Boot auto-configures one DataSource by default. For read/write splitting or multi-tenant setups, you define each DataSource manually and mark one `@Primary`. An `AbstractRoutingDataSource` then picks the active one per request.

```java
@Configuration
public class DataSourceConfig {

    @Bean @Primary @ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.primary")
    public DataSource primaryDataSource() { return DataSourceBuilder.create().build(); }

    @Bean @ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.replica")
    public DataSource replicaDataSource() { return DataSourceBuilder.create().build(); }

    @Bean
    public DataSource routingDataSource(@Qualifier("primaryDataSource") DataSource primary,
                                        @Qualifier("replicaDataSource") DataSource replica) {
        RoutingDataSource r = new RoutingDataSource();
        r.setTargetDataSources(Map.of("PRIMARY", primary, "REPLICA", replica));
        r.setDefaultTargetDataSource(primary);
        return r;
    }
}

public class RoutingDataSource extends AbstractRoutingDataSource {
    private static final ThreadLocal<String> KEY = ThreadLocal.withInitial(() -> "PRIMARY");
    public static void useReplica() { KEY.set("REPLICA"); }
    public static void useWriter()  { KEY.set("PRIMARY"); }
    public static void clear()      { KEY.remove(); }
    @Override protected Object determineCurrentLookupKey() { return KEY.get(); }
}

@Aspect @Component
class ReadOnlyRoutingAspect {
    @Around("@annotation(org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional)")
    public Object route(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp) throws Throwable {
        Transactional tx = ((MethodSignature) pjp.getSignature())
            .getMethod().getAnnotation(Transactional.class);
        if (tx != null && tx.readOnly()) RoutingDataSource.useReplica();
        try { return pjp.proceed(); } finally { RoutingDataSource.clear(); }
    }
}
```

```yaml
app:
  datasource:
    primary:
      jdbc-url: jdbc:postgresql://primary:5432/app
    replica:
      jdbc-url: jdbc:postgresql://replica:5432/app
      read-only: true
```

- Each DataSource needs its own `EntityManagerFactory` and `PlatformTransactionManager` when you use JPA — bind them with `@EnableJpaRepositories(basePackages=..., entityManagerFactoryRef=..., transactionManagerRef=...)`.
- The aspect flips the `ThreadLocal` key before the transaction starts; swapping mid-transaction is unsafe because the connection is already bound to one DataSource.
- For multi-tenant apps, key by tenant ID (`RoutingDataSource.useTenant(currentTenantId())`) instead of read/write — same mechanism, different lookup key.

`@Transactional` works via a Spring AOP proxy. The proxy consults the `PlatformTransactionManager`, opens/commits/rolls back a transaction around the method, and uses the `Propagation` attribute to decide whether to join an existing transaction or start a new one.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Transactional                                         // PROPAGATION_REQUIRED (default)
    public void placeOrder(Order o) {
        orderRepo.save(o);
        auditService.record(o);                             // joins same transaction
        notificationService.notify(o);                      // see below
    }
}

@Service
class NotificationService {

    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)  // suspends outer, starts new
    public void notify(Order o) {
        notificationRepo.save(...);                         // commits independently of outer
    }
}

// --- The classic gotcha: self-invocation bypasses the proxy ---
@Service
public class BadService {
    @Transactional public void outer()  { this.inner(); }   // this = raw bean, NOT proxy
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void inner() { /* runs in OUTER's tx, not a new one — proxy skipped! */ }
}

// --- Rollback rules: only RuntimeException triggers rollback by default ---
@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)              // opt in to checked exceptions
public void transferFunds() throws IOException { ... }
```

| Propagation | Existing tx | No existing tx |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `REQUIRED` (default) | join | create new |
| `REQUIRES_NEW` | suspend outer, create new | create new |
| `NESTED` | savepoint inside outer | create new |
| `SUPPORTS` | join | run without tx |
| `MANDATORY` | join | throw |
| `NEVER` | throw | run without tx |

- The proxy exists on the **public** method of a Spring-managed **bean**. Calling from inside the same class (`this.method()`) bypasses it — extract to a separate bean or use `AopContext.currentProxy()` + `@EnableAspectJAutoProxy(exposeProxy=true)`.
- `REQUIRES_NEW` commits to the DB **before** the outer transaction commits, so partial work is durable even if the outer rolls back — use it for audit logs and outbox writes.
- Checked exceptions do **not** roll back by default. Add `rollbackFor = Exception.class` or throw a `RuntimeException`.

**Authentication** asks *who are you?* and produces an `Authentication` object in the `SecurityContext`. **Authorization** asks *what can you do?* and checks roles/authorities against that object. Spring Security pipelines both in a filter chain.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableMethodSecurity     // enables @PreAuthorize / @PostAuthorize
public class SecurityConfig {

    // ---- AUTHENTICATION ----
    @Bean
    public UserDetailsService users(PasswordEncoder encoder) {
        UserDetails alice = User.withUsername("alice")
            .password(encoder.encode("secret"))
            .roles("USER").build();
        UserDetails admin = User.withUsername("admin")
            .password(encoder.encode("secret"))
            .roles("USER", "ADMIN").build();
        return new InMemoryUserDetailsManager(alice, admin);
    }

    @Bean PasswordEncoder encoder() { return new BCryptPasswordEncoder(); }

    // ---- AUTHORIZATION ----
    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain chain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        return http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a
                .requestMatchers("/public/**").permitAll()                  // no auth required
                .requestMatchers("/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")              // role-based
                .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.DELETE, "/api/**").hasAuthority("SCOPE_write")
                .anyRequest().authenticated())                              // must be logged in
            .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults())                           // AuthN mechanism
            .build();
    }
}

// Fine-grained authorization on a service method
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN') or #order.ownerId == authentication.name")
    public void delete(Order order) { /* ... */ }
}
```

- The **AuthenticationManager** (invoked by filters such as `UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter`) validates credentials and returns an `Authentication` with authorities. On success it lands in `SecurityContextHolder.getContext()`.
- The **authorization layer** (`AuthorizationManager`, `@PreAuthorize`) is the *only* thing that decides whether a caller may invoke a method — URL rules are coarse, method annotations are precise, use both.
- `hasRole("ADMIN")` is sugar for `hasAuthority("ROLE_ADMIN")`. Keep the convention consistent or bugs creep in (the `ROLE_` prefix is implicit only on `hasRole`).
- Never rely on the UI hiding a button as authorization — the UI is a hint, the server is the source of truth.

The OWASP Top 10 (2021) lists the most common web-app risks. Most are preventable in Spring with configuration plus a few guard rails.

```java
// A01 Broken Access Control — method security + explicit rules, never URL guesswork
@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN') or #userId == authentication.name")
public UserDto getUser(@PathVariable Long userId) { ... }

// A02 Cryptographic Failures — BCrypt for passwords, TLS everywhere
@Bean PasswordEncoder encoder() { return new BCryptPasswordEncoder(12); }

// A03 Injection — use PreparedStatement / JPA parameters; NEVER concat SQL
